[{"id": "d8ee9aa8-b6ac-4da2-9b91-cb10e5576259", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Mike Goldstein (06:52): So we've been exploring that question a lot lately over the past year and a half, especially with rebranding here and solidifying our positioning. Fuck, it is a really tough, that's the hardest question. And I think especially in this industry, not like you're dealing with, I also need to be careful how much I go into our positioning right now. But no, I think for us, one of the biggest things, or there's a couple of things, and I guess lemme start off with how we've dissected that. We started by just asking our clients why they kept working with us and why they wanted to, because like you said, there are so many production companies and we asked our Toronto agencies, why do you work with us? You're in Toronto, there's tons of companies out there that's the hub for Canada. And we got two very consistent responses. And one of them was that we were great partners with them and had really formed a really good collaborative partnership that wasn't just this quick transactional project with them. (08:12): And the other thing that came up was actually at the time, our post workflow of all things, but I think that partnership from start to finish was super important. And this year what we've been kind of dissecting from that is within that partnership we're talking from the time that we start even talking about what projects might be coming up, what's possible to the time that we finish something, I think all the companies can plan, plan and production, execute it really well and do the post on it. But I think, I dunno, we've been talking about how, for us, it's really in that almost before we get into pre-production in any kind of diagnosis and strategizing stage that were, we excel, I'm terrible at explaining this right now, but\n\nCole Heilborn (09:09): That's okay.\n\nMike Goldstein (09:11): Yeah, yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:16): So you're talking about the before pre-production, the planning is that you guys do a lot of creative development and concept creation stuff too, right?", "token_estimate": 507, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:52", "end_timestamp": "09:16", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "07aa6ebd-8080-4629-895b-7d725b73c657", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Mike Goldstein (09:27): Yeah, for our brand work, we do a lot of the creative dev on it, but even for our agency stuff that we've done, we will jump on calls even before projects are green lit and just jam even with the creative team on like, oh, what's possible here? And even for one, for Cadillac last year that we were doing, they send us this deck of this activation they were doing and they were like, yeah, we want to do maybe some time lapses of this thing getting set up. And we were like, well, hold on, hold on, hold on. Here's what's possible from the film side. Let's not do that. Let's do this. And you just start that very early stage. Again, partnership jamming on stuff together. And I think that's what's ultimately yielded, just better work through the process, if that makes sense.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:15): Yeah. I'm probably doing\n\nMike Goldstein (10:16): A terrible job of explaining that, but\n\nCole Heilborn (10:17): So the creative and just kind of being willing to have it be an open discussion across the table with your clients, you think that's one of the things that's kind separated you guys in the field?\n\nMike Goldstein (10:29): I think so. I mean, it's something that seems, it obviously happens with a brand when we're doing the majority of the creative work, but even with the agency side, you obviously don't want to step on anyone's toes from the creative department, but if you can just break down those bullshit walls there and just start actually collaborating with them, that's when I think stuff really starts to become very, very good.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:55): Yeah. Do you find that, you use the question why a lot in that stage? For your previous example, why do you want to use time lapses?\n\nMike Goldstein (11:05): No, actually.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:06): Oh, okay. Interesting. Yeah,\n\nMike Goldstein (11:08): Just thinking back to it,\n\nCole Heilborn (11:10): Just curious because found that sometimes that's a really good motivator to ask someone to defend or explain the reasoning behind why they want to do a certain thing. So just curious.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:27", "end_timestamp": "11:10", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "3e96ebc1-65e6-4263-a56b-c9c900e6c258", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Mike Goldstein (11:25): I think if we got to a point where they weren't open to other stuff, (11:31): That's definitely when the wises would come in. But thinking back to that project specifically, it's more of like, we see what you guys want to do. We're not, we're not going to be like, no, you shouldn't do that. It was like, let's do that. But also, boom, for instance, this was for a Cadillac piece that they were building this activation in a mall. And originally all we were told was, oh, it's going to be a mall activation. We were like, that sounds really boring. But then they sent us the deck for it, and the build out was this 180 degree video wall (12:03): That played all this abstract textures on it, and it changed depending on where you were touching the actual physical car. And we were like, okay, cool. This thing's really quite an amazing, beautiful visual piece. This is something that we'd honestly, you'd shoot this in studio and it would look beautiful. It doesn't matter that it's in the mall. So we were like, alright, well why, instead of just shooting some basic it getting built out time lapse of people around it, et cetera, let's try and shoot this in a studio. And we weren't able to shoot it in a studio. They couldn't reset it up in a studio. So we were like, okay, well let's just bring the studio to the mall. And so we ended up shooting overnight in the mall. We had to VFX out a bunch of stuff to make it a little more clean background on it. But in the end it was, we had this really beautiful mix of super cinematic, more stylized visuals mixed with the real consumer interaction stuff and still had our time lapses, but there's a lot more to it.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:58): Okay. So I want to back up a little bit because alluding to the idea that you guys are really deep in the creative stuff and really enjoy kicking around ideas. How does the culture of your business promote that?\n\nMike Goldstein (13:14): Dude, these are really tough questions to,\n\nCole Heilborn (13:17): I promise. I'm not trying to stump you, I'm just No,", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:25", "end_timestamp": "13:17", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "4cd537f3-01a2-4c51-aace-94d0e4ebb4f5", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Mike Goldstein (13:19): No, no, these are good. It's good to think about. I almost just want to ask, bro, winners, you want to phone a friend? I don't know. I think that's always been something that I've been really stoked on and those are the kind of people that I like to work with and bring on. And I think we're just really open to that. I think we all love collaborating with great creatives. We call out shitty creative when it's there. Yeah, I think it's just a matter of who we're surrounding ourselves with and yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (14:09): Right on.\n\nMike Goldstein (14:11): Does that answer\n\nCole Heilborn (14:11): That? No, I think that does. Yeah. It's your people, right?\n\nMike Goldstein (14:15): Yeah. Okay. If I think of anything else, I'll\n\nCole Heilborn (14:21): Drop it. Alright. What have you learned about yourself over the last 10 years or so as you've been doing this?\n\nMike Goldstein (14:38): Now, Bronwyn can correct me if I'm wrong over there, but I'd say learning to work with people better, learning to collaborate better with people, trusting people more. I think the trusting people is the biggest thing. I think for any creative, it's extremely hard to let go of control on anything, but I think if people are set up for success and you can just let go of it, it's pretty amazing to see what comes out of that. I think the biggest thing I just changed, I just want to do everything and no one that anything else did was, that's good in my mind of what I thought should be there. But yeah. And I think seeing, I don't know, what do you think?\n\nSpeaker 3 (15:38): I would say that I've definitely seen a change in the way that you approach collaborative projects, I want to say through the years. And I think that just shows in the transition between Goldstein Productions to the heist, and that's just the biggest way to visually see that change. Like you as a creative, I would say that. I would say that you're pretty lifelong. Not in a bad way.", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "13:19", "end_timestamp": "15:38", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "e61194b3-a385-464d-b592-5d43611643c0", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Mike Goldstein (16:10): No, no, no, no. It's true. I mean, even a couple of years ago, again, most of the work was, it was just me. It was a bigger freelance mic with everyone helping out. But I think seeing people grow in what happens when you kind of let go is awesome. Bronwyn's directed some killer shit this year for Tent Tree and one of our agencies in Toronto as well at the beginning, very small project, but the pieces from it are so good. And yeah, seeing that growth, you can't see that without hands offing it.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:53): Yeah.\n\nMike Goldstein (16:54): But yeah, I think that's the biggest thing I've learned. Good questions, man.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:01): No, I like that. It's a good answer. Yeah, letting go of that creative control is tough sometimes, especially I think when you are used to doing it all just out of habit or out of necessity.\n\nMike Goldstein (17:13): Yep. Yeah, there's a weird emotional attachment, I think, to a lot of creative stuff. I'm not sure why, but\n\nCole Heilborn (17:22): Do you want to dive a little deeper into that?\n\nMike Goldstein (17:24): I mean, I don't know what else to, I mean, fuck, I'm no psychologist on there, but yeah, it's definitely tough to let that go. And even learning how to even receive feedback, I think too, I remember when I received my first round of feedback for that very first Whistler project, we were working directly with Whistler, but we were getting just extra feedback from the agency that they worked with on winter stuff. And I remember getting the first round of feedback and I was like, this is all fucking bullshit. They dunno what they're talking about, blah, blah. And you just go on these tangents. But yeah, then you realize how to not be like that. And it does make all the work better,\n\nCole Heilborn (18:10): I think, when you get a bunch of feedback that maybe you're not open to or you're like, they don't get it. This is a creative piece, blah, blah, blah,\n\nMike Goldstein (18:19): Blah. Well, it feels like they're criticizing you too in some ways.", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:10", "end_timestamp": "18:19", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "35fa60b5-2dea-44a7-a1c5-757e0c0185d6", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:22): Sure. But I think that back to one of my original questions, I wonder if that's part of the difference between doing short film, personal stuff and doing commercial stuff. You have to be open to that stuff because there's a lot of other stakeholders and there's a lot of other people with more experience, with better ideas than you. And maybe I've found having to check your own ego sometimes\n\nMike Goldstein (18:48): Yes,\n\nCole Heilborn (18:49): Is tough.\n\nMike Goldstein (18:52): Absolutely. The ego side of things and learning to let that go, it's super hard, but definitely really good when you can.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:02): Yeah. So one of my last questions, if you had to start over, what would you do differently? Would you just use a totally different career? Would you just be a lift up on the mountain to ski the whole time?\n\nMike Goldstein (19:15): I'd be a pilot. No, that's the only other thing that whenever I walk by a plane every single time, I'm like, should I just drop it on? That's what I wanted. That was the very first job I ever wanted. But if I had to do this again, I don't know. I don't know. I think the grass is always greener, so honestly, I don't think I'd change a thing. It's, we've had some pretty incredible experiences and I think going back to the people that you surround yourself with, at the end of the day, I think the greatest pleasure out of this is the fact that I've gotten to do just some super fun stuff with people that I like being around every day. And if I can keep doing that, whether we're making corporate videos or the biggest Super Bowl car commercials, it doesn't really matter. As long as I think it's decently fun projects and you're working with great people, that's the best.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:16): Sweet. So we're out of time for today. Where can people find you, your team if they want to find you?\n\nMike Goldstein (20:24): You can currently find us@goldsteinproductions.ca, and then in another couple of weeks you can just go to the heist ca after we rebrand.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:35): Awesome. Cool. Well, thanks again for your time. I really enjoyed getting to sit down and chat with you. And we'll see you guys next time.\n\nMike Goldstein (20:42): Awesome. Thanks for having me.", "token_estimate": 567, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Goldstein"], "start_timestamp": "18:22", "end_timestamp": "20:42", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "0ef15a37-b9c7-42d9-be58-4ce8e9895b2b", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Chris Killen (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo, and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges, and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial, and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Chris Killen, the Global Marketing Manager at Bell Helmets. Chris, how are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:45): I'm doing well, Cole. Great to talk to you again this time. For real.\n\nChris Killen (00:51): I know.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51): On the podcast. I'm stoked, dude. Thanks for having me.\n\nChris Killen (00:54): Yeah, we've made it few technical challenges, but when your state is on fire and internet keeps going down, I guess that's to be expected.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04): Alright, well, thanks for bearing with me.\n\nChris Killen (01:07): No worries. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11): Santa Cruz, California. We're about an hour and 15 minutes south of San Francisco.\n\nChris Killen (01:16): Okay, right on. Well, I got to say, I'm excited for every guest that we have on the show, but especially you guys growing up and riding my bike road and Mountain Bell Helmets is, you guys have always been there. I think I've had a variety of your helmets. I know you're more on the motorcycle side of the business, but I'm excited to have Bell represented. We actually don't know that we've had any protection products on the podcast, so you might be the first.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:49): Well, I'm excited to break the ice. That's awesome.\n\nChris Killen (01:53): So tell us a little bit about Bell. What's the background? How'd the company get started?", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:53", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "c2f0cbbe-8495-4e3c-9855-542ffb2b200f", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:58): Yeah, bell Helmets, as it stands today, was founded in 1954 by Roy Richter in Southern California. But it was actually, it was a brand prior to that, still called Bell that created auto parts and things of the sort in Bell California, which is where it got its name.\n\nSpeaker 3 (02:26): Okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:28): Yeah. And so in 1954, Roy Richter actually worked for the Bell prior to him actually taking ownership. That's how he was involved, and he saw the need very quickly for hard shell helmets. And so yeah, he took the brand from what it was like an auto parts deal to one of the most iconic brands in the world as it stands in the Motorsports and two wheel\n\nChris Killen (03:03): Industry. Yeah, that's cool. I didn't know that background. I guess without knowing what the background was, I always imagined the name came from some connection between your head, your noggin\n\nCole Heilborn (03:15): Ring, your bell.\n\nChris Killen (03:15): Yeah, something like that. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (03:19): Exactly.\n\nChris Killen (03:20): Your version's a little better. It makes more sense. Right?\n\nCole Heilborn (03:23): Yeah.\n\nChris Killen (03:24): So what does your job entail and how long have you been with the company?", "token_estimate": 312, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3", "Chris Killen"], "start_timestamp": "01:58", "end_timestamp": "03:24", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "9bb8278f-8469-4884-a838-ce1a6f27a08a", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:30): So my job at Bell, being a global marketing manager is multifaceted, just like most people's jobs in the corporate space today, those with budgets being the way they are, especially with all this recent stuff going on. So, yeah, especially in today's political and economic landscape with COVID going on, which has been great for some companies in some industries, but it's also created a ton of demand, and that for us has been very exciting. But it also means with the family of brands looking forward, because we're owned by a company called Vista, outdoor budgets are tighter and personnel is a little bit tighter. And so my role, basically everything from every piece of content you see, whether it's a served ad via any of the social outlets or an organic post, any of that content, any of that strategy, all the way up to making sure that my distributors around the world have the content and materials and information they need to best sell the helmets.\n\nSpeaker 3 (04:54): And\n\nCole Heilborn (04:55): Everywhere in between, I manage a team of athletes, probably 60 athletes around the world. My side of the business is road racing, so sport bikes on racetracks, and then as far as athlete management is concerned and flat track racing, which you're in the Pacific Northwest, so that's actually up there. It's a big deal. There's some of the best flat trackers in the world have come out of that area, the Baker family, JD Beach, and folks like that. But then I also, I handle all of the ad buys, all of the creation of content for those ads, all of the digital strategy as far as targeting, timing, launching product. I'm sure I'm missing something, but it's a lot.\n\nChris Killen (05:53): Yeah, I'm sure in an hour when you're onto your next task, you'll be like, oh, that's right. I also do this thing too. Totally.\n\nSpeaker 3 (05:59): Yeah.\n\nChris Killen (06:00): So how did you end up in Bell? What's your background look like?", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3", "Chris Killen"], "start_timestamp": "03:30", "end_timestamp": "06:00", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "36e47436-92a1-4e3d-910c-ba86e484fb7d", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:04): Okay, so yeah, that's a great question and I'm actually stoked you brought it up because many years ago I moved to Santa Cruz. I was originally from Southern California, but I just got burned out on the place, and I moved up here and I quickly realized that there's not a lot of work in Santa Cruz unless you're in real estate or construction. There's a couple other things because kind of cordoned off from the world, if you will, the Santa Cruz mountains create a divide between us and the Valley, Silicon Valley and all of the tech space over there.\n\nSpeaker 3 (06:47): So\n\nCole Heilborn (06:47): While we benefit greatly from that money, because a tourist town basically, although it's now, it's becoming more like it's creating its own economy outside of the tourism industry. So I moved up here from Southern California, went to college for television and film production, and came up here and realized, well, there's no work in these fields, so I'm going to have to make the work myself. And so I started just filming and editing and doing digital strategy, social media, marketing, all that kind of stuff, SEO, AdWords, everything in between. And just by a happenstance, me and a friend created this video. He had this idea for the video, and I had the idea to make it a viral video. And we created what was tarp surfing. And so that's not giving it enough credit, but in the essence of time, that's what put me on the map. I was contracted by Apple Mountain Dew, and a bunch of companies just reached out and were like, whoa, who's this guy in Santa Cruz that made this video that nominated him for a People's Choice Award? Got three and a half million views. It was a big, big deal. And\n\nChris Killen (08:12): This was back when,", "token_estimate": 441, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3", "Chris Killen"], "start_timestamp": "06:04", "end_timestamp": "08:12", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "88b513c3-a0e5-4d8d-94db-2aebd02edb27", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "This was in about 2009. And (08:19): Bell Helmets is here in Scotts Valley, which is just one of the other little cities in Santa Cruz towns in Santa Cruz, and it's a small town. And they found out who I was and what was going on. We're like, Hey, we want you to do some work for us on a contract basis. And so I was a contractor for them for about five years. And then when we found out that we were going to have a baby, I reached out to all of my clients, well, the ones that I felt like I fit the mold best to go work for full time. And Bell was one of the, I received offers, but Bell was the best offer because I didn't have to move. I was able to stay here in Santa Cruz. So all in all 10 years, but as a full-time employee, five years.\n\nChris Killen (09:07): Okay, gotcha. So let's go back to 2009. That's like a year after YouTube came out because it was 2008 YouTube launched, right?", "token_estimate": 224, "speakers": ["This was in about 2009. And", "Chris Killen"], "start_timestamp": "08:19", "end_timestamp": "09:07", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "53f0a308-9c61-495d-829e-39c5f5092141", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:16): Yep. Yeah, I had the idea, I was always tripping out about viral videos, and most of the stuff wasn't really viral that we were seeing in Super Bowl ads or commercials on TV or even on social media at the time. It was paid for by a big agency behind a brand to push a message or sell product or build brand loyalty. And I thought, you know what? I can do this without all the money, but I need a good story, good music, and then a really well thought out strategy. And so I have to admit, my goals weren't as lofty as what the product ended up becoming. The content ended up becoming, I thought if we hit a hundred thousand views, which still at the time would've been mind blowing for just a little Podunk guy with an old GL two camera and super old first. (10:19): Did we even, were there GoPros then? Yeah, we had a GoPro, I'm pretty sure. And yeah, so we ended up in the first day getting a hundred thousand views overnight. And so my inbox was blowing up from YouTube and all these people that were just like, dude, your thing's going crazy. It's going crazy. And I'm like, whatever. And then I went and look at it, and sure enough, it's already a hundred thousand views and my YouTube inbox is just full of, and it just continued to do so. And that was one of the most special parts about it, was the messages from people that were like, oh, I'm landlocked in Afghanistan. I'm an airborne ranger, and I used to surf, and this tarp surfing thing brought me so much joy. And I'm a nurse in Georgia, and same story. I was a surfer in California, and man, this is so cool. You made my day. The music was great. We had this, I was heavily involved in the surf industry, and so there was a bunch of nuances that I kind of played on with, they tow people into big waves with jet skis, but we were towing these guys on skateboards through these big tarps with dogs kind of a thing. And we were just checking all the boxes, really. And so (11:45): It was mind blowing then. So yeah, I mean, companies like everybody and their mother in Japan wanted to license the footage, and so (11:53): Money started coming in. I didn't even know about monetizing on YouTube at the time. I wish I would've, because while I wouldn't have made retired, but I could have made a healthy amount of money just on the monetization from YouTube, especially at the time because their rates were much higher. And then, yeah, there was all these companies in Japan, news outlets that were like, oh, we want to licensure footage. So I mean, it became almost a full-time job in itself, just managing that process with the licensing of the footage. And then the big dogs started calling Apple and Mountain Dew because they wanted Apple launched an iMac, or no, it was the MacBook Pro in I think 2010 or 2011. And we consulted, and they had, when in the commercials, the laptop pops up and it's spinning around. It's all beautiful over that traditional Apple background and it's footage of tarp surfing on their national global campaign. It was mind blowing, man. It was really cool. It is a very special time.", "token_estimate": 776, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:16", "end_timestamp": "09:16", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "63c8f868-edc6-49fb-a039-0844f6caa995", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Chris Killen (13:03): I have so many more questions. You've brought up so many interesting points. Going back, you said that your buddy had the idea for the video, but you said, I think we can make this go viral.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:15): Yeah.\n\nChris Killen (13:17): How did you make it go viral? I think a lot of people would argue, if you want to make a viral video, you can't. You just have to. So that was\n\nCole Heilborn (13:24): My point. Yes. And that's what I sought out to do, because everybody was like, oh, you got to spend a bunch of money, or it's got to be a cute cat or a farting baby to be a viral video. And I'm like, no way, dude. With strategy, time of day targeting pr, I just checked all those boxes. I saw my release, my release time. Well, let's go back to the content. So the content, I had a strategy of having the content is very campy. It's not fly on the wall, but it's handheld, so it's very engaging. The lighting was really cool. Cinematography was cool, but I also had this strategy in those early days of YouTube where everybody was doing full frame. And so I'm like, oh, if I frame mine down, if I export out of final cut at a smaller frame size, it'll be almost like letterbox, but it's completely, there's a black square around the entire video. (14:22): And so I was like, I bet you that's something that's going to draw people in as they're looking at this stuff. And the music is Animal by this guy named Mike Snow, old, great song at the time, super good, really, really good to edit too. And so I knew that that was going to be something, it was popular at the time, it was still popular, I should say, and I knew that people could vibe to the music. And it was the whole process of, even back then, they were like, oh, your content can't be over three minutes. And I'm like, dude, I'm making this thing six minutes going to run the full length of the song, and I bet you the analytics will show that people are watching more than half of this. And so it was that keeping people engaged, but then also creating content that I knew people would share or I didn't know they would share, but I assumed that they would share because there's humor in it. There's just this kind of wild thing that's tarp surfing that my friend, Homer Ard, Santa Cruz resident, he's a midtown guy, amazing, amazing artist. And also just I guess that goes with most amazing artists is that their minds are just going. And so he had this tarp surfing thing, (15:37): And so we had dogs, we had people falling down. This was back in the, there was starting to be the standup paddlers back then too. And so we poked a little fun at the standup paddlers. And then, I mean, the strategy was the hardest part about the content and then the digital, and then the actual release strategy. So then what we did was I was like, okay, so these are the times where it looks like stuff is doing the best. But I had to pick a time for release that at the time, it was end of day, it was the end of day in New York, so we launched it at two o'clock Pacific, so that's five o'clock eastern, and then that's whatever time in London. That was my big thing in London. I wanted it to go over there in the eu. And so I knew that it was a Friday, and so that the 2:00 PM, I'd get a lot of traction out here on the west coast and in the Pacific time zone, (16:56): Because I targeted a lot of surf outlets, media outlets with it. I reached out beforehand. I said, Hey, I'm launching this video. You can share it if you want, but if you don't share it, you're going to be behind the game. Everybody else is going to, and you're not going to get the views or the traction to your website, so go ahead. We're not, but this is what we're doing. We're doing it this time. And so I knew that two o'clock in the afternoon in the corporate space on a Friday, that's half day people are already, they're just sitting at their desk waiting to go home, like, look thumbing through YouTube or Facebook. And so I was like, that's good. And today in New York, same thing. People are getting ready to boogie out of the office, one last click of the whatever emails coming through, press release from whatever surf website. That was actually kind of the most interesting thing still to this day, is I only targeted surf outlets because that's all I really knew at the time, and that's all I had contacts for. And then on the Facebook and the YouTube side, it was very much about driving people back to the YouTube channel. I wanted YouTube to be my home where everybody went back to. And what's wild is that today, that's where it's going back to YouTube long form videos. It's kind of going back that way. And it's so cool to see that. I'm getting a little off your question here, but as far as strategy and how we made it happen, but", "token_estimate": 1203, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:03", "end_timestamp": "13:24", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "8081573d-a4f6-4830-be32-cecbe07463de", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Chris Killen (18:31): Well, it sounds like you reached out to PR or brands got press releases organized, and you just said, Hey, we're doing this thing, and you got it out to as many ears as you could, and then just hope that it would\n\nCole Heilborn (18:45): Catch on. Exactly. That's really what it was. Yeah, that's what it was. I knew that the content was good, but I didn't know. You never know how people are going to receive something. But I knew it was good. It was strong,\n\nSpeaker 3 (18:56): But\n\nCole Heilborn (18:56): I also wanted, because I was nobody, and I'm still nobody in the social media space, my job is making sure that the brand is in a good spot. So I knew that between me and my friends, we couldn't make it anything super special. We could do something where all our friends saw and yeah, yeah, that's cool. Slap high fives. But yeah, I had to reach out to, I reached out to some high level surfers, all of the media outlets in the surf industry, some snow stuff, some skate stuff, but mostly surf. And yeah, it was a well-rounded approach. And yeah, I went to, honestly, what's crazy is that we released it at three o'clock or whatever it was, and I didn't even check it until the next morning just because I didn't think I was maybe a couple thousand views by tomorrow, and it had already just gone. Absolutely Banana\n\nChris Killen (19:58): Man. So you launched this video. It went viral partially by accident, lot by design, it sounds like, and then brands started reaching out to you, apple and Mountain Dew. Were they looking to license the footage? What were they wanting to do with you?\n\nCole Heilborn (20:18): They basically just wanted somebody to be there on set for their production that created the video or had a part in the video so that the tarps were a certain way, the angles of the content\n\nChris Killen (20:33): Were Oh, so they were recreating what you guys had done?", "token_estimate": 478, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "18:31", "end_timestamp": "20:33", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "699ce51b-3890-41be-8ab4-ab589d549943", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:36): Yes. Okay. And it was actually very, very kind of them to, because they also hired some of my friends who were the talent in the video to come shoot as well. Oh, cool. They could have, they're such juggernauts, they could have just done it themselves. And we had the idea 10 years ago, these guys saw it on our whatever. And so yeah, they reached out. They reached out. And it was via YouTube as well through the messenger.\n\nChris Killen (21:07): Crazy.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:10): Yeah.\n\nChris Killen (21:11): Interesting. Man, that's so long ago. Just in terms of internet and videos and content, how looking now, we shot\n\nCole Heilborn (21:21): Everything on tape dv. It was all, dude, you know crazy, right?\n\nChris Killen (21:27): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:27): How old are you?\n\nChris Killen (21:28): I'm 26.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:30): So you never even shot on, you never shot on tape, did you? I'm\n\nChris Killen (21:33): A digital baby.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:34): Yeah, dude, so we were shooting all on tape, man. Oh my goodness. Yep.\n\nChris Killen (21:39): I feel like I can't really call myself a filmmaker if I've never shot on film. It feels a little wrong, but I haven't shot on film.\n\nSpeaker 3 (21:47): No.\n\nChris Killen (21:48): So in today's climate, analytics and data and strategy is become a thing. Obviously from 2009 to 2020, almost 21, YouTube's analytics have changed immensely in terms of what type of data you can find about your consumers. I'm curious from your perspective, as someone who's been interested and involved with strategy for a long time now, how do you feel about the phrase content is king? Because so many people talk about making content and just getting content out there. What's your take on that?", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Killen", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "20:36", "end_timestamp": "21:48", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "4cd2f493-860a-4c54-b25c-41fa45197137", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:26): It's almost like what I just said was that content is king if it's got a strategy, but you can't, could pump out content all day, every day without any strategy and the content, while maybe a filmmaker can look at it and be like, oh, that's beautiful. That's such a good piece. But the content, it's not going to go anywhere. Especially in today's day and age where algorithms, algorithms change daily. It's like you're almost set up to fail really as a business or a brand when you're not to fail. But they don't want you getting as many views organically as you should because they want you to spend money boosting and promoting your posts. And so again, content is king only if it's married with an equally thought out strategy.\n\nChris Killen (23:27): So strategy is king and content is queen, something like could be.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:32): Yeah, exactly. Or king and king or queen and queen, one without the other. To me, in my experience is just, it's a waste of time. I've seen, there are a few examples that I've seen where a $250,000 production budget goes into something, and then there's the strategy is an afterthought because whoever came up with the content said, or the idea for the content and the strategy for that, for building that content that, oh, dude, this is so good. It's just going to go viral by itself. And I've seen stuff, I mean, that truly is brilliant and amazing and wild and helicopters chasing motorcycles and all this stuff and crashing, but it gets 800 views and it's because there's that content for whatever reason, is getting quez at the time or squeezed through through that algorithm funnel.\n\nChris Killen (24:41): Interesting. So you can talk about strategy, and I feel like there's a lot of directions you can take. It obviously depends on the content,\n\nSpeaker 3 (24:49): It", "token_estimate": 461, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Killen", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "22:26", "end_timestamp": "24:49", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "eb7529eb-d91d-4816-8684-929370d1a21d", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Chris Killen (24:49): Depends on the message, the audience, how as someone who's been doing it, what are guidelines? What do you use to set yourself up for success? When you start thinking about strategy, think about someone who's never thought about or knows much about strategy. What are some guidelines they can think through as they're trying to figure this out for themselves?\n\nCole Heilborn (25:14): Those guidelines I would suggest for a strategy around content that would best line someone up for success or that content for success would be first setting a goal or goals, a series of goals as far as who you want to see it, how many views you want, where you want it to be seen, how long you want it to be seen. And then also roll that over into literally hitting all the bases. And so it's like, okay, so right now, if I was going to put out content, but this could change tomorrow. I mean its not for most, for all intents and purposes is not going to, but I would host something on YouTube. I would share teasers on if those teasers are reels or IGTV or whatever throughout all of my other social platforms that are all driving people back to YouTube, my blog, a k, a website for bell helmets, there'd be a post on there, the community page that kind of has banners so that when people are shopping around the site, they see this banner for this, oh, what's this thing? (26:34): What's this new piece that they're putting out? Or what's this new product that they're telling a story about? And then serving ad, and then also the targeting on the, so I would marry an organic and a boosted post or posts, I should say, because a big thing that I like to do is ab test content versus whether it's identical content, different targeting, different copy, different hashtags, different whatever. And you send those out into the world, see which one's doing best, figure out why or not one of 'em is doing better than the other. And then either change one that you think will outperform the one that was outperforming it prior, or just dump the entire budget that you had budgeted for this project into the better performing piece with a better performing copy content, all that kind of stuff. And so it's a very, while we did a post a while back, a campaign a while back called The Day in the Life with one of our riders, and he's a professional stunt motorcycle rider, very high profile, super cool, but for the average person, they can't associate with that, they can't associate with doing 180 miles an hour on a motorcycle to a dead stop on the front wheel and then setting the back wheel down and then wheeling out of that off into the sunset. (28:19): Most people are like, Hey, I ride a motorcycle just to go get groceries. I don't go over 35 miles an hour. I don't go on the freeway. And so the strategy really started for that entire campaign before any of the digital stuff was the story. And it was telling a story about he had had a daughter at the time that was, I think two years old and just going through a day in the life with him, really cool guy, Richie edited and shot framing where it's like the coffee drawer slides out and it shot overhead and the editing and the music, the sound effects are all there. Boom. Coffee pot going in, this and that, like the gas turning on, baby, getting up, airplane, some baby food into its mouth and saying bye to the wife, hopping on the bike, heading out to the gym kind of a thing. (29:14): Making it just more of something that's attainable for the average person, because especially on the street side of motorcycling, any of that stunt stuff or professional road racers is something that most people are never going to be able to touch or do in their entire life. On the dirt side, it's a little different because it's a cheaper entry point. People can get into riding like dirt track or motocross for way cheaper. And there's a track basically anywhere within 50 miles, a hundred miles of most people. They can all go out. And so the level of entry is a little bit easier. But going back to the street side and this content, it was really about making it one, I wanted the people, I just wanted the people to consume the content in its entirety. And then throughout that, we were talking about this, our new technology for this shield called pro tint, and it's a photochromic face shield that goes on a helmet that you ride around on the street and it automatically adjusts to changing light conditions. (30:36): So the shield tint. So that's the cool thing about it is in this piece, there's this guy, Aaron, he leaves his house. It's five 30 in the morning. There's little cool timestamp things. We had all these cool little call outs and stuff and showing the tint of the shield, but we did it subtly and we weren't really kicking it down people's throat. We did it more so it was more mission impossible, just these little hits with cool little music or sound effect. But yeah, throughout that thing, you see the shield changing, going from clear to dark and back to clear to dark. And so that was a way that we were able to make a piece of content that was not only brand driving, but also volume driving. You hope, you never know if people are going to have $149 to afford just the face shield when they could buy a helmet for $99 with a shield already on it. Of course theirs doesn't change, but I will say that we don't have any of those shields in stock. So miss an accomplished.", "token_estimate": 1365, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:49", "end_timestamp": "25:14", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "85ee5160-2c6d-4bef-823e-4be7fec56a83", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Chris Killen (31:44): Yeah, there you go. How has strategy, and I know this is kind of maybe a too istic of a question, but how has it changed over the last decade in regards to digital marketing?\n\nCole Heilborn (32:01): Well, as you spoke to earlier, the analytics portion of it has almost trumped like the actual almost strategy itself, because that information is so valuable. Maybe it doesn't stand above it per se, but I definitely think that strategy is, there's this hand in hand thing where, okay, so 10 years ago it was very strategy. A lot of it was PR based, and a lot of it was still print based. And so that's obviously gone out the window. And then even those endemic outlets that switched from print to digital, still people started realizing that if they could, don't tell anybody this, by the way. I'm just kidding. But\n\nChris Killen (32:58): You're speaking to a room full of people right now, Chris,", "token_estimate": 230, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:44", "end_timestamp": "32:58", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "c2309ca6-cefc-492f-883c-d17aac31c73b", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:00): I know know if anybody, if any brand as much as a brand can be direct to consumer in their sales funnel, their marketing, their whatever, that's the best way forward. And so the endemics, they're dying because brands are realizing that they can advertise throughout their own outlets, social platforms, their own blog, if you will, website, to much greater success into a much more highly targeted audience (33:40): With Facebook allowing with the pixel function on Facebook and lookalike audiences, the more their brand is moving forward in those spaces, the more their strategy is changing away from working outside of their house of social media platforms. They're not advertising as much with the endemic outlets really at all. And so that means that the PR aspect of strategy is, again, I know that there's still a place for pr, right? Of course, there's always going to be a place for pr, but there's going to be, from what we've seen, it's gone from every PR agency in the world is a PR agency, and now every PR agency in the world is a content creation house, (34:31): And they're not very good at it yet they still charge a premium. That's super frustrating. And so yeah, the strategy and then as far as just even if you're sticking within your own social platforms, strategy is changing because the algorithms are changing. And it's like, okay, so Instagram and stories are, now, we're taking a step back in time, but Facebook, well, yeah, that's the big dog. Okay, Instagram's coming up. Instagram is now the big dog, and Instagram also has this story function. Facebook's going to try to do stories. So Instagram is putting their IGTV and they're favoring content throughout IGTV. And so then Twitter is basically just fallen by the wayside for any real advertising for the, I mean, it's still a viable source, but I think it's only for politicians and stuff like that, but I'm just kidding. (35:38): And so you're constantly keeping up. And then so okay, TikTok comes around, you're like, oh, we should advertise on TikTok. Oh, tiktoks funded by the Chinese and this and that. This is rumor mill, dude. Don't take this as me preaching that TikTok news as gospel about the Chinese owning it and they're stealing information and blah, there's like this, whatever. But then Instagram goes back and ups to their auntie and says, okay, we're doing reels now and we're offering this music function. And so the strategy has changed because the social media platforms and the landscape in which we advertise changes so drastically. And the way to stay ahead of it is just to always be doing it.", "token_estimate": 656, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:00", "end_timestamp": "33:00", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "6beff203-e405-46fe-b218-fbd41d83dda0", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Chris Killen (36:22): The platforms we use are always going to change. One day YouTube will die out and something else will take its place. But I think what never changes is the fact that behind all the strategy, you're really just trying to communicate to people and people, well, our people, and certainly maybe we change over time, but for the most part, I got to assume our motivations and our interests and perspective on life stays pretty much the same or evolves really slowly. So maybe Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, all these new platforms keep popping up, but you're still just talking to people.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:08): Exactly.\n\nChris Killen (37:09): And I think maybe it's just understanding regardless of the platform you're on, just understanding that people are behind. It's all\n\nCole Heilborn (37:16): Totally. And that's a great point. And it is something that we personally, I very much pay attention to that and listen to that and understand that. And that was why I had the whole message of solidarity thing go out was like, man, I'm scared and I still have my job. What about the people that don't have a job, but they're home hoping that their resume gets accepted somewhere else? And I know that people are glued to their devices\n\nChris Killen (37:50): Until\n\nCole Heilborn (37:50): They watch the social dilemma. Then they just turn that thing off, throw the toilet. Did you watch that?\n\nChris Killen (37:55): Yeah, it's good,\n\nCole Heilborn (37:56): Dude. Very good.\n\nChris Killen (37:58): Scary. Very scary. Actually, we haven't recorded it yet, but I am trying to schedule an interview with another marketer to talk just about that documentary.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:10): Awesome.\n\nChris Killen (38:11): I mean, that's a whole nother five podcasts right there.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:14): That is so cool. Well, I'm looking forward. Can you actually just shelve mine and get that one done? I want to hear what you guys have to talk about. I'm being serious. I can't wait to see the email come through subscribed to you. So I can't wait to see that come through. I really, really want to listen.", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:22", "end_timestamp": "38:14", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "2789810a-a5d0-4477-ac21-9914eadbc309", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Chris Killen (38:33): Well, yeah, I'm not sure when it'll happen yet and when it'll come out in relation to this episode, but that's a good plug right there. If you aren't signed up for the newsletter, well totally. You should sign up for it.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:45): Absolutely. It's great. It's great content. And go out and watch the social dilemma, and you will be listening to a lot more podcasts than thumbing through your Instagram feed.\n\nChris Killen (38:55): Yes. From my perspective, and I'm curious, I'm using you as a sounding board, so let me know your thoughts on what I'm about to say. In the production world in film, what I love about it is that all we're doing is telling stories, and I feel like the companies that are doing really well and will continue to do really well are the companies that continue to tell stories. Totally. It takes people like me, and it takes people like you who understand the strategy and the content to do that really well. Do you think that we're going to keep that more and more companies will become these storytelling media brands? I don't.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:31): No, I don't. I hope that they do, and maybe they will, but if today is any indication, it seems like a lot of brands are just comfortable reposting and not telling their own stories, and they're telling their ambassadors stories. And I understand that that ambassador, I mean, we have ambassadors and athletes that tell the story of our brand, but while I hope that what you're saying is true and that brands continue to tell stories, and there are some brands out there that are very good at telling stories like Yeti, incredible dude. I mean, that'd be such a good job. And there's a lot of brands in that outdoor space that are actually doing the same, that are telling very good stories. But then on the other hand, I'm seeing massive brands. All they're doing is just they're not really telling any of their own stories, they're just reposting stuff that people are saying about them. And you can tell that they're not really paying attention to the\n\nChris Killen (40:32): Why is that, obviously, I'm assuming it's not a resource problem.", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:33", "end_timestamp": "40:32", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "4d1e35ce-22b0-42e0-bef1-6488a9cc7286", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:37): So that's one of the most interesting things about the corporate space is that people get complacent sitting at a desk job and they got a paycheck that's coming in every two weeks. I mean, I'll give your listeners my address if they want to come fight me on that one, but take offense to it. But I see it every day. I see that. And so I know that there's a lot of complacency. It's very much easier. But then, so I will argue that resources are another part of the problem, (41:17): And that is that brands, like there's a lot of brands out there that are just like us. They're squeezing down, they're clamping down, and so they don't have maybe enough people. They've got one person, a 2 million follower social media platform. And while that's doable, that's pretty hard when if you're a 2 million follower brand on Instagram, that means you probably, and you're in the space on motorcycles, that means you probably have a hundred athletes racing around the world on any given weekend. And so there's content coming in from all different avenues and that kind of content that needs to be looked at and studied and okay, this is a good one to repost or whatever. But then the other thing is just, I think it does come down to budgets. (42:24): I think about this all the time. The death of brands as they grow bigger and bigger is that you're absorbed and bought and purchased over and over and over, and every time you purchase that person who's bought you, while they think that you're a good investment, they also have to start off making that investment look good on paper for that next year or two years or three years, whatever the deal is. But they're cutting people, they're cutting headcount, they're cutting budgets, and so budget cuts. Some people think like, oh, all we got to do is just share stuff on Facebook. All we got to do is share stuff on Instagram. (43:06): There's a couple of brands out there that I look at, and they're literally sharing almost every 30 minutes, 2 million fans, 2 million followers on their Instagram. They're sharing, let's not say every 30 minutes, but every hour and a half they're sharing something, 2 million followers, fans, and they're getting nine likes. Like, dude, why are you not looking at this? How do you not see that? Are you not looking at anybody else? Are you not looking at, well, helmets, we have about a 10th of the size of their followers and audience, and we like five, 10, 15,000 likes. Massive, massive engagement and impressions.", "token_estimate": 631, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "40:37", "end_timestamp": "40:37", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "82720feb-afb9-42fe-bcae-94395f73d7e8", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Chris Killen (43:51): Interesting. One of my last questions for you. We're running out of time. What are the biggest challenges, or what are your blind spots right now with Bell? What are you guys not doing very well of that you could improve on or, I dunno, I'm just curious, where do you guys need to grow?\n\nCole Heilborn (44:08): Totally. So are you talking about the business in general or are you talking about our strategy and marketing\n\nChris Killen (44:17): Side? Yeah, maybe just more of what you could speak to from your wheelhouse.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:20): Okay, cool. Thank you. Then I won't get in trouble. No, actually, we're doing great things as a brand. Like I was saying, we have great brand recognition, great brand loyalty, awesome products. But as far as the marketing side, I'll go right back and take the easy way out and just say that if I had a magic lantern or magic lamp or whatever, and I could rub it and have a genie come out, and even if I only had one wish, it would be more people to help with social media and then also more people to help with content creation.\n\nChris Killen (45:06): Okay, interesting.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:09): But social media would be, and social media, and those would be the big things, I believe, as unfortunate as it is for advertising outlets and PR firms and the things of going direct to consumer, all the writing on the wall says you should be doing that. So social media investment as a team, finding people who are really, really good with Pixel, I wish. That's one thing. So I'm just as guilty as the people from this brand. I was talking about 2 million followers. While there are steps back, I'm steps ahead of them, but still, I just don't have the time to really, really dive into Pixel. So I wish I could have just a person dedicated to Pixel.\n\nChris Killen (45:58): Yeah, I mean, you're ahead of these guys, but there's someone else who is ahead of you.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:03): Exactly. Right. And so yeah, if it was anything, it would be just a heavy investment in social media.\n\nChris Killen (46:10): Gotcha. Cool. Well, Chris, thanks for chatting.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Chris Killen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "43:51", "end_timestamp": "46:10", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "6a889bb8-6927-40d9-a496-26d8595158cc", "episode_id": "44194880-32e4-4782-8c27-8e0b90adf369", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484c", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (46:13): You're so rad, Cole, this is so awesome. Thank you, dude. This is very, very cool.\n\nChris Killen (46:17): Yeah, no worries.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:18): Excited.\n\nChris Killen (46:19): Thank you for spending the time and chatting. It's cool to hear your background and hear how a kind of random tarp video in the early days of YouTube has led you down this crazy career path where now you're hanging out with motorcycle athletes and\n\nCole Heilborn (46:36): It's so crazy, man. I'm practiced a lot of gratitude, especially with a five-year old. I like him to do the same, and I'm grateful every day for that tarp surfing video, and it's such a funny thing to be grateful for, but literally, my horizon went from right here to it's endless mean, but everybody's like that. Everybody, it's like our perception of ourselves is tough. We all think, oh, I'm just stuck in this rut. But man, take a deep breath of fresh air or create a tarp surfing video.\n\nChris Killen (47:17): Yeah, you just make your own tarp surfing video and a little strategy behind it.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:23): That's all you got to do. No worries. No, just content is king, dude. Just put it out there. It'll be fine. Give you a million views.\n\nChris Killen (47:28): My favorite is when someone asks, so how do you get YouTube to show up and make a video,\n\nCole Heilborn (47:34): Dude? Right. Hey, I appreciate it, man. I appreciate it, Cole. Thank you.\n\nChris Killen (47:41): Yeah, thank you, Chris. All right, man. Well have a good rest of your day.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:44): Okay, buddy. You too.\n\nChris Killen (47:45): Take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 457, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Chris Killen"], "start_timestamp": "46:13", "end_timestamp": "47:45", "podcast_index": 40, "episode_name": "Ep 40: Chris Killen | Strategy is the new King | Bell Helmets", "slug": "ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-40-chris-killen-strategy-is-the-new-king-bell-helmets-8d131", "guest_name": "Chris Killen", "guest_title": "Global Marketing Manager, Bell Helmets", "categories": [], "short_description": "Is content still king?", "podcast_length": "44:62"}, {"id": "4ba73a48-c8f5-49f9-89fc-6364b3f29b0e", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hoborne. Today I'm sitting down with Amanda Goad, the co-founder of Bold Brew and Founder of Project 16 x. Amanda, how are you?\n\nAmanda Goad (00:47): Great. Thanks for having me. Excited to be here. We've been talking for a while, so excited to finally be on the show.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:53): Yeah, no, it's good. I'm excited for you to join us. Yeah, it's been a couple months I think we've been trying to coordinate, but it's finally all come together and this morning we're here to chat. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nAmanda Goad (01:05): Durango, Colorado. So the Sunny Dry San Juan Mountains.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:12): Okay, right on. I'm curious, what's the background on how the San Juan Mountains got their name? Because in my neck of the woods we have the San Juan Islands.\n\nAmanda Goad (01:21): Honestly, I'm guessing it's from some sort of saint or troubadour coming through. I think that's probably similar for your San Juan Islands, but I haven't really looked it up. Historically, they were the more, the Native Mountains were of the Ute Mountain Tribe, so I know more about that side of the background of the San Juan Mountains than I do the more colonized history of the San Juan Mountains.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:52): Gotcha. Okay. Anyways, that's completely off topic, but so tell us a little bit about Bold Brew. You and your husband both founded the company, what do you do?", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:52", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "c5b46838-c42d-48bb-8633-9312472af2e8", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Amanda Goad (02:06): Yeah, so we founded Bold Brew nearly seven years ago when we moved to Durango and we had a little background in the Durango area and both had a background in communications and marketing and wanted to find a way to have a job here in a small town of 17,000 people. And so seven years ago or so, I think it was just when this concept idea of being able to work remotely was getting a lot more capable and available to people's lifestyles. So instead of trying to find a job as a barista here or a lift for the winter, we decided to start Bold Brew. And I've previously worked at a larger agency in Wisconsin, which is where I'm from, but when I left that agency, I had zero intentions of leaving with the mission to start my own agency. But my husband and I took some time off to travel up to Alaska and pretty much saw the entire state all the way up to the Arctic Ocean and down to Homer and everywhere in between, everywhere a car and over landing car would take you because we're really into overlanding and also did some backpacking around the area (03:33): And then made our way down through all of these Western states and landed in Durango and didn't have a big goal of I have to be in marketing again, I have to be in communications again. But we really gravitated towards certain people we met on that trip and the stories that they told us. And so as we were deciding what risks to take for our careers, because we had already taken these other risks in life, traveling up to a place like Alaska together with at the time a boyfriend and not a husband. And so all those relationship risks and everything else, we decided that the thing that really tied all of those risks coming to a good success or some opportunity at the end was these stories that we heard and this passion we had for the people we met and their stories. So that's why we decided to dive into marketing and it was a combination of hearing this amazing story and then also utilizing these great outdoor products. (04:54): So I remember the first time we ever got a really good sleeping bag, we were in Bozeman, Montana using this is the middle of the summer camping in Bozeman, Montana, and we were freezing. So we went to the REI and bought a $300 Marmot sleeping bag and slept in it for the next six months on this trip. And it was like gold to us. And so although we were definitely always outdoorsy and had seen value in high quality products, we were never big gear heads needing to have the latest and greatest, but we really saw value in quality and in products that lasted a long time and got you through whatever experience and story you were involved in. And so a combination of those two things really led us to push into the outdoor industry pretty quickly as we formed Bold Brew and Durango.", "token_estimate": 701, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "02:06", "end_timestamp": "02:06", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "b7b18d6b-a05e-4514-aa85-b2814b216517", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:09): Okay. And Bold Brew. How do you describe yourselves now?\n\nAmanda Goad (06:14): I would describe us as a PR and marketing agency that focused on brand story and voice for brands in the outdoor wellness lifestyle and philanthropic arenas.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:31): Okay. And did your husband, what was his background? Did he have any interest in marketing or why did he want to be involved?\n\nAmanda Goad (06:39): Yeah, he is actually the entrepreneur, guru side of things. He's very fiscally savvy and really is great at running that side of everything with Bold Brew, but his background was in science and laboratories and research on that side of things. But I think he's always had a knack for entrepreneurship and really digging into how businesses start and grow and flourish. But he recently told me that I thought was interesting is that he loves the social side of business too, and I think that he loves the social side of business more than I do.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:29): Oh, interesting.\n\nAmanda Goad (07:30): Having meetings with a client, getting in front of people and building new relationships and building authentic relationships that shows outdoor retailer, he shines at Outdoor retailer much more than I do. He's always forming really authentic, long-term new connections and I love that dichotomy that we have in running and building bull brew.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:56): Yeah, that's got to be a great, if you guys kind of equal each other out or that's got to be a good dynamic.\n\nAmanda Goad (08:04): Yeah, I mean owning a business with your husband also a parent to your parenting at the same time and significant other definitely has its challenges, but at the end of the day, I can't imagine it owning a business with anyone else.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:20): Yeah. So I'm curious also, you said you're really into Overland. What type of overland rig do you have?", "token_estimate": 475, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "06:09", "end_timestamp": "08:20", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "723ef853-c269-4ea6-b369-f4f24f18bb71", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Amanda Goad (08:27): Several. Oh, okay. So we went to Alaska in a 2011 FJ cruiser. That was Adrian, my husband's car at the time that he had recently invested in. And so it was fairly new, only a year or two old at the time. Since moving to Durango, we have also gotten a 73 FJ 40 and an 87 FJ 62. So those are our over landing rigs. I feel nervous to say we also have a Jeep that we take over landing, but I'm on the Toyota side of things. We just happen to, that's more of our commuter car with kids and we take it on easier trails too. So yes, we have a lot of cars, but we don't drive them all that much. So we try to stay economically and environmentally sustainable in that way.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:35): That's awesome. Do you guys run rooftop tents? Do you throw tents on the ground?\n\nAmanda Goad (09:41): We actually don't do rooftop tents because they have a tendency to have your car tip a little bit or so on harder trails. They make your car a little top heavy. I used to want one so badly and recently the harder and harder trails we go on, the more I realized maybe it's not the best idea. And also with a toddler and another one on the way, being up in rooftop is probably not the best arena. So regular tens and sometimes we sleep in the back of the FJ 62, which I love. It's nice and cozy.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:19): Okay. Alright, well completely off topic, but I was super curious. So tell us a little bit more about some of the clients that you work with. Are there any recent projects that you can kind of talk about that give us a good idea of how you approach a project or work with a client?", "token_estimate": 403, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:27", "end_timestamp": "10:19", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "fd4b7d26-5c18-40c4-9560-09aec7420932", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Amanda Goad (10:35): Yeah, so one client that we've been really excited to work with recently that we've had a relationship for a long time through various networks is Coalition Snow. And we just did a rebranding on a visual level for them. So telling their story on a visual level, not necessarily on a verbal or copy language level. And that included visual assets for their fall campaigns, redoing their website, how they talk about their ambassadors and their programs, and then bringing that into all of their promotions and doing PR for it this fall. So I was really excited to work with Jen Gki, the CEO at Coalition Snow because she and I have mean anyone that knows us in the outdoor industry know that we've been friends and we've spoken on panels at Outdoor Retailer together for quite a while and we've worked together on and off in the past. But really being able to dig into an amazing women focused brand like Coalition Snow and seeing Coalition snow evolve over the last four or five of knowing Jen and then being able to take that gradual evolution of seeing their story evolve and seeing Jen grow, seeing Jen be really big part of the Bipoc community and the Jedi community and a strong voice and leader in those communities and being able to kind of bring that out in a visual level on their website was really exciting this summer.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:16): That's a perfect plug and I'm glad you did it so I didn't have to, but if you're curious, Jen has an episode that's come out before your episode, so folks are interested to learn more about Jen and Coalition Snow and all of the rad stuff that they're up to. You could go listen to that one. You mentioned that Bold Brew focuses on building brand voice and helping with visibility. I think actually I stole that from your website. In your mind, what is brand voice? I'm curious talk about brand storytelling, brand voice, but maybe let's define some common language around what a brand voice is in your opinion before we keep moving.", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:35", "end_timestamp": "12:16", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "c23e042a-882e-49ee-a66c-3be7f4e50e3f", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Amanda Goad (12:58): Definitely. When we are approached by potential clients or brands, we're often approached at a level of, I need PR or I need this social media campaign, or I need to push more people to my website. And that's all that the brand knows. And we work with clients from all sizes, from small, new emerging startups in the outdoor industry and lifestyle industry to larger established brands like Sherpa Adventure Gear in the outdoor industry. And when we talk to these brands, they just, sometimes there's just this goal of the end game, but they haven't really established who they are\n\nSpeaker 3 (13:51): And\n\nAmanda Goad (13:52): What their voice is to the world and establishing that voice and that story really differentiates you from everyone else that you potentially competing with in your immediate arena and then other potential markets as well. So my favorite part of marketing and strategy is really digging into the gaps with clients and potential clients of you have this amazing, incredible product. You are doing this awesome new thing that is helping sustainability in the world, and I never even knew I needed your product until I started using it or things like that, even on our Alaska trip. But then being able to share that product with so many other people requires you to talk about it and to talk. You need to have a voice. We all have voices when we speak. And so I think building a voice really builds community and connection in order have longevity to your brand in the world that you're trying to build for your brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:11): Gotcha. Yeah, well said. Do you have an example of a client that you've helped unpack what their brand voice is? Just to give an example of maybe something tangible we can work with?", "token_estimate": 443, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:58", "end_timestamp": "15:11", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "ae42321d-68e8-4375-8077-146802de7fbf", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Amanda Goad (15:25): So when we first started started Gold Brew, we worked with another startup that was emerging that's much better known in the outdoor industry now called Western Rise. And we had a really strong relationship with Western Rise helping them evolve their voice from where it was in the beginning to where it is now. And so that took them from, they started Western Rise because they wanted to have a product that they wanted to use out on the river when they were fishing. And the founders of Western Rise are really big anglers, and you'll still find them out on the river all the time. They're based in Telluride here in the San Juan now. And they were originally in Georgia when we started working with 'em. And so we started working them from the ground up of what their voice was at the time and why they established their product and their business. (16:29): And we were with them for two and a half years along the entire process of what their voice evolved into as their business evolved. So they started looking at new audiences and realizing that the product that they were actually creating was much more versatile than just for fly fishing and that you could use it out on the trail or traveling or heading onto an airplane or anything of that nature. And so they wanted to appeal to a larger arena and they continued to evolve their voice from there. And they've definitely done a really big pivot from what they originally launched at to who they are now, but they've taken time and been thoughtful about it throughout the entire process in establishing this pivotal voice of who they are for the long run. And so I think that's a really good example of loving the product and loving what you're creating and why you're creating it for the world, and then building that into who needs your product and why, and then how to speak to them on their level as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:47): Yeah. So you're talking about helping them craft their brand voice. If you can give us a brief look into your process, how do you figure that out and how do you know when you've identified the voice, what does that look like?", "token_estimate": 536, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:25", "end_timestamp": "17:47", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "40ae3b5e-34ed-42c6-8910-4fa27120359b", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Amanda Goad (18:05): Yeah, lots of meetings with a client, lots of conversations. I mean, I think in any type of storytelling, whether it is establishing a brand from the beginning or doing public relations or sharing a marketing campaign, you have to really dig into and get to know the brand and the client. So it's not easy to just say, hi, nice to meet you. I'm Amanda from Bold Brew and now I'm going to help you build your brand voice and I don't know anything about you. So I think that establishing a really strong relationship with your clients and getting to know them and feeling like you are a part of their brand and not an agency, working for them is crucial. And then digging into who their audience is and who their audience could be. And then that from a tangible tactical standpoint, that can evolve into anything from consumer surveys, market research, digging into competitor audits, diving into mood boards and potential colorways. And so there's a lot of more qualitative tactical things and then quantitative tactical things behind it. And I really love how it all works together in collaboration. And our team is skilled and good at different variations of it from the much more creative side of things on our team to much more tactical, quantitative, let's look at the numbers and how we grow this business and where my role comes in a bold brew is how that all works together.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:59): So you kind of pull all the strings, bring it all into one cohesive PDF, for lack of a better word.\n\nAmanda Goad (20:04): Yep. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:05): Okay, interesting. So you help them find their brand voice that's kind of like the foundation maybe? Yes. Am I on track? Yeah, definitely. Then where can they go from there?", "token_estimate": 440, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:05", "end_timestamp": "20:05", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "970a5747-2ef2-4e41-8ac7-0696b28e71b1", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Amanda Goad (20:21): Then it's a matter of telling this voice and story to the world and where is the best place to tell that voice and story? So sometimes it can be social media and how are you telling it on social media? Sometimes it is through people of influence, whether those people of influence are on social media or they are people in networks and arenas that your customers might be living in all the time. And they're not necessarily of influence online, but they're of influence in their own social environments. And sometimes it's working on traditional public relations and building that with some non-traditional methods too, and what PR looks like these days.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:16): Gotcha. So let's say I've got a business and I want to publish something on social media. I have this message or this, I dunno, this concept and what I should do is consider running it through my brand voice, which is kind of like a filter, and then that gets distributed to whether it's social or publications or YouTube. So it's all consistent.", "token_estimate": 262, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:21", "end_timestamp": "21:16", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "00ec7e23-75b3-4c02-99e4-c86fb4318985", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Amanda Goad (21:46): Yes, cohesiveness and consistency is key. Definitely. We always tell our clients, don't just put a social media post out there or don't just jump on a bandwagon. You see something happening in the news. Be thoughtful about how you're reacting to the world in situations and definitely make reactions when they need to on a timely level, but also make sure that everything you're doing is consistent with the brand that you've shown and shown up for. And then you are continuing to do that moving forward, past whatever reaction or promotion you have just in that time. So I think a really great and timely example of that these days is with the Black Lives Matters campaigns and everything going on in that world, a lot of brands want to show support for that. But if you haven't authentically been showing people of color in your imagery in the past saying that you want to photo shoot with black models or native models and not really bringing them into the culture and community of your company for the long-term and making sure that they feel included and that your company has an exclusive inclusive values and inclusive voice is not being authentic to that voice you've created and established. (23:23): So that's just one example of making sure the voice that you create is always evolving, but making sure that it is clear to who you are trying to talk to moving forward. So if you are just making sure that you aren't jumping into something without your voice sharing, that you actually do have that authenticity and that value.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:01): Yeah. Yeah. So I feel like brand storytelling is kind of like that next step you have to have in order to tell stories through your brand. You have to know who your brand is and have a brand voice brand storytelling seems to be kind of this next cool thing to do for marketing, but does it have to be such a big undertaking? Can it be small and done on the small scale or does it have to be these large initiatives? I guess what's your take on that?", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:46", "end_timestamp": "24:01", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "fe82894c-a3ae-4521-9ae2-d831ab6cc0a6", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Amanda Goad (24:36): Yeah, I think that historically in marketing, storytelling has always been a part of a brand and I think that it's this sexy keyword that people are using now to sell their business or sell their services or anything they're doing. But overall, for centuries and centuries, we as humans gravitate towards stories that are engaging and tell us about a community or a place and give us an affinity and shared values to something that we want to also connect with (25:23): And have those same values for. So I think that you can tell your brand story and the story of others that represent your brand in so many different mediums in ways, and that can be anything from a gorgeous, beautiful, lengthy YouTube video that Yeti creates and those are fantastic, and they're really engaging to a post sharing your influencers and your new ambassadors on your social media pages, or maybe it is utilizing Instagram TV once a month to authentically interview your ambassadors or have them do takeovers. Maybe it's utilizing traditional PR methods to have a publication interview one of your ambassadors or athletes and tell their story, and in turn, they're telling your brand story. So those are all different forms of mediums that you can get your story out and this values behind your story and voice out, but I think the key is making sure you're sticking to that storyline and that value system in anything you do.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:42): Gotcha. So yeah, I guess I'm trying to clarify. Obviously stories have been a huge part of human civilization since forever, but you'd said that brand storytelling has been around for a long time. Has it though? I feel like brands, companies historically have just been focused on products and features and not so interested in the story. I don't know.", "token_estimate": 452, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:36", "end_timestamp": "26:42", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "be0429b8-dc4f-4ac1-94b8-22f1d69d9486", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Amanda Goad (27:11): I think maybe from a consumer level it looks that way, and maybe I've dug deeper and kind of seen that the consumer is wanting stories more than looking at features. I think historically, if you look at the way a product has been promoted, for instance, the first thing that comes to mind is women in the fifties in their very traditional domestic lifestyles and being promoted through black and white television the way a new product is going to make their house make their life easier by helping them clean their house or get dinner on the table. Those are all specs and things that can happen through that new product, but the story that those brands are telling is you want to be this strong capable wife that does everything for her family and they're sharing a value system for that time (28:27): Of the value. And I think within that, and I'm not super familiar with lots of different promotions that have been on during that time, but within that they've even used things like celebrities, which at the time could have been considered influencers for these types of things. Maybe in a sense of utilizing the word storytelling these days we're, the direction we're talking about is really diving into influencers and not always focusing on a traditional athlete or celebrity and digging into the everyday person and hearing their story and a general consumer gravitating towards that person that they can really relate to, and the fact that brands these days will tell a story about an influencer and not even mention their brand the entire time and at the end through their logo up, that's definitely new and different and I think that it's super impactful these days to dig into featuring people of the same value systems of yours in order to build that connection and community.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:43): Gotcha. Okay. So brands and companies have been telling stories, it's just stories have evolved and how they're telling them, is that kind of what I'm hearing?\n\nAmanda Goad (29:53): Definitely, yeah.", "token_estimate": 509, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:11", "end_timestamp": "29:53", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "550ee105-1717-4b72-a00c-698f2aaca3bf", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:54): Okay. I never thought about it that way because from the production side, I think of a story as an amazing Yeti film, but then you look at an ad from the fifties where they're selling a new dishwasher, I don't really think of that. It is a story, you're right. But I never thought about it in that way.\n\nAmanda Goad (30:09): It's a shorter story. So the medium's changed in how the story's being told, and I think the way Apple brew, the way we dig into the story and voice is like, who are you? What is your voice? What are your values and connections? And then we work with companies like yours to build out a more in depth story to create an amazing video. And that is a different definition of the word story because you're really digging into a hunting film of someone. I've got hunting on the mind since it's hunting season, so a hunting film or I saw a really great Onyx app film recently where they're starting to do a lot of similar storytelling that Yeti has done. I think a lot of that arena is utilizing that method and it is great because a hunting season is short, so you don't really get to live and breathe the brand and the products that you want to use during that season the entire time of the year. So these brands have to find ways to keep their audience engaged with people of similar values and community throughout the entire year. And then they do that through these stories. You're talking about these productions and these stories where literally the Onyx video was a story about a hunter and boundaries, and so there was some thematics going on with their app and everything, but they didn't talk about (31:50): Their app throughout the entire video and then at the end through their logo\n\nCole Heilborn (31:54): Up. So maybe what we need is a whole series of podcast episodes to story and how stories have evolved.\n\nAmanda Goad (32:03): That sounds great.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:04): Yeah,\n\nAmanda Goad (32:05): We need to do some college research on some of that. That feels like it could be a whole class.", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "29:54", "end_timestamp": "32:05", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "f3a55e5a-51b1-4821-a242-b6848435d5ef", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:11): It does. And so speaking of the Yeti style, which I think is funny how now that has become an attainable or people are working towards, oh, I want a Yeti,\n\nAmanda Goad (32:21): And it's called that, it's like Tissues are Kleenex, the Yeti\n\nCole Heilborn (32:25): Film. Yes. Do you think we're going to see more of that? I mean, obviously there's a lot of restrictions that go into being able to do something like that on the scale of which they do.\n\nAmanda Goad (32:36): I think we have seen more of it in lots of different outdoor arenas. I think that lots of brands have done a really good job at taking that concept (32:50): And putting it into new mediums too. So putting it into shorter videos that have really high production value still for Instagram or for IGTV or even thinking about how you can take that production quality and that story quality and put it into a campaign that might include a handful of different types of mediums. So a post for Instagram, an IG TV video, and then the athlete or ambassador or influencer who is featured in it to actually be talking to the person on a personal level in some capacity. I think that that is the next wave of this storytelling is using the production value when it makes sense and when it is of value to certain aspects of a campaign, but then also including other aspects of a campaign in there that people might say are lower production but are very relatable to a consumer and are catchy and engaging. I believe one that I recently saw that I'm thinking of when I'm seeing all this was Patagonia, and it was Carolyn Gly and Brooklyn Bell telling people to get out and vote, and it was a phone call video between the two of them, a video chat. (34:28): So I think those types of things paired with slightly higher production videos throughout larger campaigns are what is the next wave of this Yeti type storytelling?\n\nCole Heilborn (34:43): Interesting. So you've got the hero anthem like pretty video, and then you've got maybe the behind the scenes where the talent is answering questions from the audience or more of a personal selfie type video, something like that", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "32:11", "end_timestamp": "34:43", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "5cbfcd04-2e23-4808-839a-70e3fea4ef5a", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Amanda Goad (35:01): Definitely where everything goes together cohesively. I'm sure you've seen the video that Jimmy Chin has recently promoted to get people to get out and vote and that I know this podcast will occur well after everyone has voted, and that video was this beautiful storytelling, lots of different shots, but then they've attached smaller campaign assets to that too.\n\nSpeaker 3 (35:34): And\n\nAmanda Goad (35:34): I think that that is key for any type of storytelling production moving forward.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:40): Yeah. So you mentioned you think we're going to see maybe production value, production quality being used more selectively. When does it make sense for that to happen?", "token_estimate": 176, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:01", "end_timestamp": "35:40", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "0858526e-7f61-436a-9fe4-cd800091cec5", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Amanda Goad (35:55): I think that people always want to see high production, but one thing I was thinking about as we're talking about this is you don't, just because something isn't high production in a sense of you're using the best camera and you're filming for how many days and things like that doesn't mean that there isn't a lot of creative thought and energy and strategic planning put into something like the video Patagonia did with Caroline and Brooklyn. And so I think that when it comes to any brand telling their story, making sure that strategic thought and creativity and planning is still very strong, and that's high value in whatever content you're creating versus quick one-off content. And then along with that, your traditional high video production will be a part of campaigns when they make sense. So I'm taking a stab and a guess here based on some insider knowledge, but for Jimmy's video that he had about voting, he had some content that he had probably filmed throughout things. (37:17): It was definitely a montage, maybe some of the content was new, it was definitely high production, but I know for a fact some of that content was bought from Getty Images because I was in the video. Were you really? For a split second from a video shoot I was on a few years ago with a film friend Kylie Fly, and it was like a film that production shoot we were doing, and then some of it was sold to Getty Images. And so I just think that there are lots of different ways to create those videos. That story was still being told very highly impacted and really strong, but it was a montage of Jimmy's content and other things that they grabbed throughout the outdoor industry or I'm sure you're familiar with a lot of the social content that's happened recently where people are asking people of influence or anyone in general to video them talking about a specific topic or answering a question. And then there's been lots of montages created from that, and that is a lower production value content, but there's still a lot of thought put into the questions and the creativity behind things. And then I think pairing those types of content with these larger production campaigns is what grabs people's attention in the long run.", "token_estimate": 562, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "35:55", "end_timestamp": "35:55", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "166271c7-a588-40e6-b8e6-a150141ac90b", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:58): Gotcha.\n\nAmanda Goad (38:58): Because at the end of the day, you don't want to just see tons of Yeti like videos and no social content. Well, maybe we do watch a lot of those on YouTube at our house too in the hunting arenas or Overland, but then you don't want to see just all the social type content either. And from the Yeti overland side of things, we're big fans of bloggers and YouTubers that will do more intermediate type content, I would say, because it's not super high production, but they've got their intro reels and they are making great content on YouTube. And so I think it's a balance of all of that type of content to tell the right stories to the right people. And you also have to remember that you're talking to different audiences. So you love high quality production content. I wonder why, but there might be other people that are not as engaged in it or it doesn't engage, they get lost too quickly or it's too long for them, so they need something a little shorter.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:15): Well, my wife is a perfect example. I'll sit down and geek out and watch Patagonia and Yeti films back to back and fill some films and then she'll sit down on the couch with me two minutes later she gets up and I'm like, why are you not sucked into this? This is so good. But then I'll come into the room the next day and she's watching something that I wouldn't consider beautiful from just a film technical level, but she's just loving it. I'm like, I don't see this. So yes, there's so many.", "token_estimate": 383, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "38:58", "end_timestamp": "40:15", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "b4fdfe9c-ae68-42b4-8c7d-c30b78153f6d", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Amanda Goad (40:48): It's about that community and connection. And so there's something about the voice and the brand story of something she's watching that she's connecting with totally from a video. In the last couple of years, Adrian and I have watched the YouTube show Lifestyle Overland, and they're growing YouTube show about overlanding. And the reason we first got invested in them was because they were going on a trip to Alaska with their daughter. And at the time I think we had a newborn. So we were at home bored watching the show a lot, reminiscing about our trip to Alaska, and then also thinking about the future of going over landing with our young daughter. And so that's a story that we immediately gravitated towards, and the production of that story didn't really matter to an extent it does matter because they have an intro. I've actually seen them evolve their production over the last couple of years to be higher production so that production does matter and it also builds your voice. (42:08): So one of the things behind their production that I find interesting is they do tons of technical content of rock crawling and finding campsites and stuff, but then it's also got an emotional element to it. So they will do a lot of B roll of the sunset or cooking at camp and put really emotional connecting music to it. And so that brings a different audience into what they're creating and the story that they're trying to tell so that different types of people will gravitate towards their voice. So I would say their brand voice is like this adventurous couple family that is really into the technical elements behind their two Toyota cars that they take around now. And even the mom has her own Toyota and gets really into the technical elements of rigging out her car, but then they have this emotional building memories as a family and connecting with the land and stewardship to the land type of element. And so those types of things right there would be building that voice and them sticking to it with whatever stories or mediums they use moving forward.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "40:48", "end_timestamp": "40:48", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "0113b09c-6f24-4d98-ae7f-760cec89c202", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:30): So we're running out of time, but I got to assume when you look at any company out there or you watch any film or you look at any Instagram posts, if they're not one of your clients, are you already breaking down their brand voice and categorizing it and looking at their color schemes and kind of creating it all mentally without even knowing who they are?\n\nAmanda Goad (43:55): I think that's how you stay solid at your job and what you do. It comes naturally to want to do that, but it also is fun to do that with companies that aren't clients. I think that's how we found new clients is we've seen companies that need that to be broken down in a sense of needing opportunity and done that in our heads and on paper and then approached them and it's then they've become clients, which is fantastic. And I also think that it's with brands and arenas that you're passionate about hunting and overland and the outdoor industry, it's just fun to see and it's fun to watch and see people doing new things or different things versus also seeing people telling the same stories or not telling a story and just talking about a product like you had said before,\n\nCole Heilborn (44:54): Does that drive you nuts? Like, oh, there's so much opportunity that was missed.", "token_estimate": 321, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Amanda Goad"], "start_timestamp": "43:30", "end_timestamp": "44:54", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "52fc1baf-081f-4198-9dc7-7da4a215198a", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Amanda Goad (45:01): I think that there's, as a consumer, I mean I buy products in the outdoor industry and things like that. There are two things that pull me towards a product and one is the quality of the product. And if a product is high quality and I know nothing about the story, I need to know about the brand values and what they do as a company and things like that. But even if I don't see them promoting a high production story or telling something in a way on Instagram, I'll still buy the product if it's of high quality and the brand, a brand that has the same values as mine. But I am immediately drawn to buying a product and staying loyal and having affinity for that product if there is a story behind everything as well. And I would beg to say most people out there might say the same thing. They might give companies and brands a little bit of a wiggle room in their product improvement category, their product being able to improve. If they're telling great stories, working with great people and they have really strong values, then it's time to utilize your customers and the community you've built to make your product better so that people do want to stick around with it.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:33): Yeah, well said. Alright, well if people want to follow along with you with the work you do at Bold Brew or the work you do with Project 16 x, actually really quick, why don't you fill us in quickly on what that project is.", "token_estimate": 362, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:01", "end_timestamp": "46:33", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "df043f9d-7b30-4370-b645-781ebe473984", "episode_id": "dc0aecca-5c24-4d22-a819-cc29c7387f06", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484f", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Amanda Goad (46:46): So Project 16 X is a women's leadership summit. That Bold Bruce started about four years ago. This would've been our fifth year of it if COVID wasn't around. And convening at a high country hut in an intimate way wasn't not possible, but basically it's a way for women in the outdoor industry to find ways to meet, connect, and find value in working together in the long term. So the initial viewpoint of it, or the reason it started almost five years ago was that I wasn't with a lot of women in the outdoor industry and finding ways to connect with women in the outdoor industry. And I wanted to build stronger relationships with women that I knew were leaders or had the potential to be leaders in the outdoor industry. So it kind of has really evolved from there and I am excited to see it continue to evolve in 2021 because I think there's a lot of work we can be doing with the Bipoc and Jedi communities and there's a lot of evolution that will just take place based on the chaos that our whole world has lived in for the last nine months or so. So it'll be an interesting evolution of this women's summit, but at its core, it's about finding ways that women and female identifying people in the outdoor industry can work together.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:24): Great. Where can people follow along with any of your different projects\n\nAmanda Goad (48:30): At Bold Routine on Instagram, bold routine.com? My Instagram is Amanda Marie Goad, so any of those. Project 16 is Project 16 x. So any of those arenas, feel free to say hello and we can talk about the evolution of story more\n\nCole Heilborn (48:54): Or find you somewhere on an overland trail Rock crawling.\n\nAmanda Goad (48:59): My personal Instagram is either photos of Overland Hunting or My daughter.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:05): I wonder what your daughter will grow up to do.\n\nAmanda Goad (49:09): Well, she already comes home from daycare and asks for elk as a snack, so I think that's a good sign. Parenting goals right there. I'm done.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:19): Alright. Well Amanda, thanks so much. Have a good rest of your day and take care.\n\nAmanda Goad (49:24): Yeah, thanks for having me. Have a great one.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:26): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 609, "speakers": ["Amanda Goad", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:46", "end_timestamp": "49:26", "podcast_index": 41, "episode_name": "Ep 41: Amanda Goad | Building a Brand Voice | BoldBre‪w‬", "slug": "ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-41-amanda-goad-building-a-brand-voice-boldbrew", "guest_name": "Amanda Goad", "guest_title": "BoldBrew", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Make sure your voice is always evolving, but make sure it's clear who you are trying to talk to moving forwards.\"", "podcast_length": "44:63"}, {"id": "49e0c74e-9b1c-4435-a8b1-7f0a4a43bb0d", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Jo Salamon (00:02): So we take those things incredibly seriously. We have a very passionate team of people incredibly dedicated, who work really hard to make sure all those standards are met. I think one of the things that the whole company has that I felt is that we all promote, wear it. It doesn't save your life if you don't wear it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by, for Slide Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Joe Solomon, the PR and communications manager at Mustang Survival. Joe, how are you?\n\nJo Salamon (01:13): I'm good, Cole. Thank you very much. Happy to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:15): Likewise, I'm excited to be chatting today. You're just calling in from up north up in Vancouver, right?\n\nJo Salamon (01:20): That's right. I work for Mustang Survival. We are based up in, well, Burnaby, a suburb of the Vancouver Lower Mainland, so in Vancouver in Canada.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:30): So before we dive in deeper to Mustang, I'd love to kind of talk a little bit about your background, how you ended up with Mustang. Can you give us the highlight reel?", "token_estimate": 425, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:30", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "cf086c6d-46bd-423b-a307-f9857c73e4ef", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Jo Salamon (01:39): The highlight reel? I originally started in journalism a long time ago when I lived in the uk, so I worked as a broadcast journalist there for about nine years. I immigrated to Canada and I didn't go straight back into journalism. I kind of wanted to take a break from it and ended up working in an outdoor store and loved it and sort of really kind of loved the whole community around the outdoor industry. And from there, being in Vancouver, we are very fortunate. We've got a lot of really great brands based in Vancouver, in the outdoor industry, and I mean, that's even growing since I've been here, which has been great. And so then I ended up moving into working for arctix, and this is back in 2005. So they were still a pretty small company back then. And I worked in the customer service department for about three or four years, and then eventually sort of migrated over into the marketing department and into back into sort of PR and communications. So taking my journalism and twisting it on its head and working on the PR side of it as opposed to the journalism side of it. And then spent the rest of my time working at Arc Alteryx. And then I've been at Mustang Survival now for a year and a half, just coming up to about a year and a half, nearly two years now.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:03): Okay.", "token_estimate": 332, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:39", "end_timestamp": "03:03", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "c158879c-0202-46b3-9828-22cb7de20ebc", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Jo Salamon (03:04): So Mustang Survival is a 50-year-old company, kind of started back in, I think, 1967, I'm going to say originally in Gastown, which is, if you know Vancouver, it's right down on the docks, it's right on the water. So they started with IRV down there making floater coats. So Irv started, he wanted to make an insulated jacket, and he was introduced to FOAM as an installation property. And then somebody was like, oh, and it floats. And he's like, it what? It floats. And it sort of opened up a whole new area for him that he hadn't initially considered when he was looking into insulation, but he decided to sort of move in that direction. So the Mustang Survival floater coat or the classic flotation coat that you'll see, it's iconic here on the Pacific Northwest. It's probably iconic across Canada for anybody who works on the water, in the water, over the water, commercial fishing, commercial water, anything. The float coat is a synonymous iconic orange jacket that keeps you warm, that you can work in and obviously has flotation properties. So keeps you safe on the water as well. The long-term goal being for any company that is involved in lifesaving products like PFDs, not just Mustang Survival, but we wanted a PFD to be seen a bit like helmets are now in the ski industry. You see somebody on the ski hill with a helmet on, you don't think twice about it anymore. (04:46): In fact, you look at the people who don't wear helmets and think they're crazy. And the same with PFDs. There's a lot of regulations around them, but a lot of people have them on their boat or on their paddleboard, but they don't actually wear them. And really at the end of the day, they don't save your life if you're not wearing them. So for us, and for all brands and all companies who make PFDs, our goal is that people wear the PFD at the end, you wear it. And so we try to work to make them light, to make them comfortable, to make you almost, to make them invisible so that you can just do your stuff, go fishing, go boating, go paddling, not even think that you've got it on, and just know and trust that if the worst case happens, it's there and it's going to do the job for you and safe when you need it most. That's kind of the plan.", "token_estimate": 564, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "03:04", "end_timestamp": "03:04", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "ac1db521-061b-497e-b70e-31a1f729d846", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:40): So yeah, tell us a little bit about your role currently. What do you do? What do you oversee? What are you working on?\n\nJo Salamon (05:45): So it's one of the fun things about going back to working for a smaller company again, is that you become the jack of all trades again. But I quite like that. It's fun. So my role is PR and communications. The PR side of it obviously is helping promote the brand and the product into the rec industry, the outdoor rec industry. And then the communication side evolves from everything from blog content management, working with the digital marketing team on emails, working with internal communications and brand communications and business communications as well. So it's a bit of everything. We are a 50-year-old company, but the marketing side of it is very young in the company, I'd say like four years old because the company has only just started to move into the outdoor rec side. So previously, most people know us and understand us from a commercial and professional products. (06:44): Mustang survivor has a long history of working with commercial fishermen, professionals who work in on or over the water, be that military to Air Force. We've worked with nasa, it's a big long history, and the last four years, they wanted to take all that knowledge and know-how that we were putting into a lot of these very, very specialized products. And we're like, how can we make paddling better? How can we make sailing better for people? How can we make other water sports on a more recreational level, more comfortable, safer, easy to wear products, keep you dry and allow you to really have a great time outside, playing outside. And so the brand is sort of, I wouldn't say split, but it's definitely sort of increased its design and its engineering and its marketing to focus on the outdoor recreation industry at the moment. And that's kind of partly where I came in the last year and a half to help them promote those products that we were making into the outdoor recreation industry so that people would see Mustang survival from a different angle. A lot see us like you do. They remember their orange PFD at the lake, at the cabin with the canoe, the seahorse on the logo, or if they've known anybody who works in commercial fishing, they know the floater coat, they know the gear, they know the salad pets, and they would probably try and wear them. But now we're like, okay, now we're making them with a recreation activity intended use as well. So we're making it a little bit more specific for an end user in the recreation", "token_estimate": 650, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "05:40", "end_timestamp": "05:45", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "1af35dec-6094-4216-bc85-6d21b0c7fcd5", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:23): Side. But it's only been in the last four years that you guys have focused in on those three categories.\n\nJo Salamon (08:29): Yeah, yeah, yeah. Very small. So moving into the recreation market has been sort of a place where the companies wanted to go but finally decided, no, this is where we need to focus. Let's do it. This is the time to do it. The time was right. It's everything with all companies. It's all about timing, getting the right people in, getting the right stuff together, and then being able to make it and push it out there. So\n\nCole Heilborn (08:55): Within the last four years, you've kind of gone through this pivot. What type of challenges have been associated with this new focus?", "token_estimate": 182, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "08:23", "end_timestamp": "08:55", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "28d66abc-92ef-4837-83f9-662583a83c59", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Jo Salamon (09:03): Yeah, I mean, I've literally been there a year and a half. But yeah, you look at the market, you have to see what's out there. You have to see what's been provided to those different markets. Look at any gaps that you see, look at and talk to those end users who are at the pinnacle of their sport, whether that's an ocean racer, an offshore racer, somebody who's adventure paddling and taking paddleboards and kayaks on bigger adventures. You'll see that a lot more right now. Adventure paddling is pretty huge. We work with professional bass fishermen, so these are people who spend their whole life out on the water bass fishing for tournaments at a professional level. They're out in all weathers and what do you need? What problems do you need us to solve for you? So we kind of work with them, we talk to them, we find out what they need, what they're missing, and then we worked on providing those key things using all the knowledge and know-how we have of making, basically coming from making dry suits that we've made for the military. And so we took that understanding of how to pattern and how to sew and seal waterproof breathables and just take it to a different end use. And we kind of focus on three different categories because we have the ability to do that. We don't just have to focus on fishing or sailing or paddling. We're a full outdoor water sports recreation company. We can cover the whole remit because of our background in commercial,\n\nCole Heilborn (10:39): Because\n\nJo Salamon (10:39): Of our background in what we've done for other people in the past.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:44): Yeah, I think you guys have a GoPro video on\n\nJo Salamon (10:47): Hunter Land\n\nCole Heilborn (10:49): On your website. And after we had chatted the first time I went and watched it and oh my goodness, for anyone who's interested, you should go watch the video. But these two guys are in a bass boat, they're flying down a river and something malfunctions in the boat, turns 90 degrees in half a second, and they're\n\nJo Salamon (11:11): Going and they go the opposite way just as fast", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:03", "end_timestamp": "11:11", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "23e78551-752c-4c3a-9d84-f3435680daec", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:14): At 30 or 40 knots, and they just get thrown into the water. And I think that's, seeing that moment happens so fast, and being someone who every time you go out on the water, it's so easy to make the excuse, well, if I need to put on my life\n\nJo Salamon (11:29): Jacket the worst that could happen,\n\nCole Heilborn (11:30): What's the worst that could happen? Or if I need to put on my life jacket, I'll have plenty of time to put it on. Not\n\nJo Salamon (11:36): Always, no,\n\nCole Heilborn (11:37): No, it's not that case. So I think it just goes to show that you guys are out there providing lifesaving tools to people. What type of responsibility comes with that for you personally and for the company?", "token_estimate": 182, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "11:14", "end_timestamp": "11:37", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "bd1e484a-5342-420b-9c7e-43970cb0332f", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Jo Salamon (11:51): There's a huge amount of responsibility. Obviously anything that is a lifesaving product has regulations and certification. It has to go through and pass without a doubt. So we work very closely on all the UL certifications on anything. We're making sure all our PFDs, their Coast Guard or Canada Transport approved. Currently we only sell within Canada and the us, so we don't need to have European standards just yet. As we move eventually grow and move into Europe, obviously we'll need to work on CE certifications and stuff like that as well. And everybody's slightly different Coast Guard to transport Canada. The nice thing is Transport Canada Guard are now working on a lot of what's called harmonization of PFDs, allowing you to move between borders wearing the PFD, so that you know, especially where we are in Vancouver, Seattle, the ocean, the border is pretty close to move through, but you have to make sure that the PFD you're wearing is certified in both areas and things like that. (13:00): So there's a lot of testing that goes into it. A lot of protocols we have at the office, we call it the Water Life Studio. We have a full on lab for testing everything from fabrics, bladder strength, durability. We have a washing machine that's constantly going. We have put things into stuff with rocks for durability. They're testing so many things all the time to see how they stand up to abrasion, wear and tear. We have a pool at the office too, and so we can do a lot of buoyancy testing ourselves to make sure things are writing you in the right way. So with a lot of PFDs, everything's about bringing you back up. So your head is above water, and so you're always trying to make sure there's lots of very specific engineering and mathematical calculations that have to go into how it works and how it works for body types and trying to get things to work on as many body types as possible to create the right scenario. So yeah, there's a lot of work and a lot of attention to the very tiniest details that go into PFDs. So we take those things incredibly seriously. We have a very passionate team of people incredibly dedicated, who work really hard to make sure all those standards are met.", "token_estimate": 557, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "11:51", "end_timestamp": "11:51", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "aff059c9-14bf-4da4-bb56-b0d806bf0a34", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (14:27): Is that responsibility from the product manufacturing side, the quality control side, does that similar type of responsibility extend into your team in the marketing department somehow?\n\nJo Salamon (14:40): Yeah, I think so. I think we come in and it's an old tagline we have, but it's right above the door of the building and it's like we save lives for a living and that's what we do. And that's where Mustang survival started flow to coats, PFDs, their lifesaving products. That's what we do. We're now moving more into sort of a recreational apparel line. So yes, our fishing jacket doesn't save a life. Our Callen sort of breathable, waterproof, breathable jackets. They don't save lives. But we still have that same attitude towards it that we want to help make the activity you're doing as comfortable as possible. So the drier you are, the more comfortable you are, the less likely you are to sort of make stupid decisions. You start to get cold or all sorts of things like that. But yeah, we have the same sort of attitude towards it, and I think everybody in the company has the same attitude to, I know I did as soon as I joined, so I'll take it from my perspective, I should say, as somebody who came in from a non ocean, I'm a paddler. (15:49): I'm not a sailor. I like paddling. I like being on the water. I have always worn A PFD, my comfort level on the water. I need to have A PFD. I know that about myself. So I have no qualms about putting a PFD on whenever I'm on the water. Other people feel super confident on the water. They've grown up sailing, they've grown up paddling, and they maybe feel a little more complacent about wearing A PFD. I think one of the things that the whole company has that I felt is that we all promote wear it. It doesn't save your life if you don't wear it. That's at the end of the day, it cannot save your life if you don't have it on and if you don't have it on correctly. So we all promote that amongst, I think our friends, our colleague, I know my friends who paddle with me now all wear their PFD, so I've at least managed to influence that many people. (16:48): And that's what the company does. That's what our marketing does. We try to always promote safe water use, safe water experience. We're very conscious of that. So even you'll see it even in our photos, we want to make sure that people are wearing PFDs. If we are using marketing photos of somebody on the water or in the water or near the water, that's sort of a policy we have. It's a policy that a lot of brands who work in the industry that in the PFD industry have too, that if their marketing doesn't support it, why would somebody, they're following you, they're following you for a reason, they're seeing your images, they need to see that you are promoting, wear it. You're promoting this safe space on the water. So we do that a lot. Obviously that's a key focus for us.", "token_estimate": 731, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "14:27", "end_timestamp": "14:40", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "c1468455-6e2b-4b0f-aa5b-e43d5f179274", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:34): Yeah. So how do you encourage the general public to wear A PFD?\n\nJo Salamon (17:38): I was going to say we take part in a lot of events and campaigns and support campaigns that support safe boating. So take for instance, may is usually the beginning of, I'm going to call it boating season and water season for most of us Memorial Day weekend. And everybody gets back outside. And so we have safe boating month in May, which is a lot of stories that are focused around being safe out on the water, making sure your kids are safe on the water, making sure your PFD has been checked for the season.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:13): Got. Okay. So how did your role with Arteric help prepare you for this role with Mustang?\n\nJo Salamon (18:20): I think from my perspective, I am looking at it at different activities, but the general side of how you market and how you PR is the same as we were moving more into an outdoor recreation side. My job and my role is to help promote the brand and the products with an audience that might not be used to seeing it in that activity and saying, Hey, actually we also make jackets for paddling. We also make a new PFD that's really comfortable for paddling. We also have a pair of really great shorts that are kind of like a waterproof, breathable that are intended for sailing, but oh my God, they're awesome when you're like standup paddle boarding too. And so just bringing brand awareness to a new audience and saying, Hey, have you checked us out? We actually make waterproof breathable jackets now. And they're not a full on floater coat with foam in them. They're actually a waterproof, breathable jacket. In fact, that's a question I get a lot is like, this is jacket float. No, this one doesn't.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:24): So as you described, Mustang has a really pretty beautiful heritage and history with the company. How do you preserve that while moving into this new category, this new recreation focus?", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "17:34", "end_timestamp": "19:24", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "66aed66b-2b06-4353-af54-3a8c30ca75e7", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Jo Salamon (19:37): Yeah, that's something I think we're still working on a little bit. So currently our marketing focus has been to partner with our ambassadors and end users and we share their adventures and their storytelling where they're using and taking our products out on their adventures. And that's kind of what we've been doing. It's been a slow growth, very organic, very much about how everybody else is using our products to go and have adventures to, as we say, to live beyond land. And what does live beyond land mean to you. So we've been sharing that out. We're actually at that point right now where we are looking at, okay, how do we bring in that history and the heritage of who we are and what we are and bring that out and share that a little bit more into our recreational marketing as well. (20:32): Something we're just slowly weaving in, I think, as we go along. And then the hard side for me from a PR perspective is we have so many amazing stories to tell really. I wish I could sit here and tell you some of the really cool stuff we do, but a lot of the stuff we can't talk about. And unfortunately their government contracts, they're contracts we can't mention, we can't talk about them. But what's really cool is the innovation that we work on in those contracts slowly comes down to the rec side. We don't see it, you won't see it, you won't understand it, but we get to go, oh, you know what we could do with that? And then we work that into something that we could use on the rec side, which is amazing and it's great. So even though we can't always talk about some of the contracts that we've had in the past and some of the stuff we've done or the partnerships we do with other brands, we can sometimes find awesome pieces of innovation that we can move into the outdoor recreation side of things, which is great.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:39): So circling back to that idea of taking the heritage and communicating it to the rec community, what have you guys tried? What are you looking forward to trying? What's working, what isn't working?", "token_estimate": 518, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:37", "end_timestamp": "21:39", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "1b00e871-a2b0-4f85-aada-efdb39bc4469", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jo Salamon (21:50): So we're very fortunate that a lot of people who do know of Mustang Survival and know Mustang Survival know it from its commercial side, from its history and its heritage. They already know about that. They already understand that and they see how well it's been trusted and used by commercial professionals, whether those professionals are Alaska crab fishermen, whether they're people who are working on oil rigs, whether they are people who have to fly over water for their profession. We do a lot of work with the Air Force, whether they're search and rescue teams, swift water rescue teams. You'll see a lot of swift water rescue whenever there's an area where there's hurricanes and floods. You'll have swift water rescue teams in there who are helping search and rescue with police. So you see our dry suits being used there, and these people go to us first and foremost because they can trust it. (22:49): It does the job. They have the confidence to carry on and do their job. And because a lot of people have seen that already, that story is kind of there. It just maybe isn't sort of vocalized as much. What we try to do now is weave it into our storytelling on our social channels, into our blog storytelling. So we've got swift water rescue stories. We talk about some of the other uses of our products or where they've come from. We have a story going out next year, which is all about the history of the inflatable PFD belt, the one that all the paddle boarders are using. Well that comes from the Navy Seals and it comes from the Navy and the Navy use. And we have this sort of history of how we've worked with the Navy on creating an inflatable belt vest for them that we took into paddling and into the paddle sports industry. (23:45): And so we have those kind of stories, history and heritage stories. Next year there's a story that we're going to try and talk about the innovation of PFDs in general and highlighting one that we make actually for the military and for law enforcement, which is amazing. It's so tiny, it's like less than a pound in weight, but it inflate and bring somebody up to the surface with all their gear on. It took a lot of engineering, it took a lot of problem solving to create this piece, but it allows them to have one less piece of equipment that's as heavy or as bulky and it can keep it out of the way. And that innovation moves into what we wear. So you'll see in the M mi T 70 that came out sort of this year, we were able to make a much slimmer, thinner, lighter weight PFD that you can wear all day and not notice you're wearing it anymore. And you can have an automatic or manual version. And we're able to do that because of the work we do with governments, with the commercial side that we work on. Innovation can trickle down into everyday recreational use, which is great.", "token_estimate": 714, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "21:50", "end_timestamp": "21:50", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "d97f71ac-44ac-44db-a17b-4eb21e399ba3", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (24:54): Yeah. So what are you most excited about with this new direction for yourself personally?\n\nJo Salamon (25:00): I think what we can achieve with the products, we are bringing out a women's line for 2021, which we're all really excited about. There's a women's dry suit in particular that we've focused the line around, and it's a dry suit specifically made for women for their use of wearing a dry suit, not guys. And we wear things differently and we need to be able to use the dry suit and get out of the dry suit in a different way. So there's been a lot of really great design gone into that so that when we launched it, we were launching a really beautiful, functioning performing dry suit, specifically designed just for women to allow them to go out and have their adventures. It's tough because as a smaller brand it's always hard to bring everything out at the same time, what to charge ahead and put everything into everything in one go. But we had to do it slowly. We've done it slowly and now it's sort of ramping up. Over the next couple of years you'll see a lot more of the apparel side sort of growing and developing, which is really great.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:07): Well Joe, thank you so much for being on the show. We're running out of time, but I wanted to do a quick plug. I know you guys work with Big Fish Collective, Rachel Pop on some of these new products that you guys are launching. We actually had her on the show\n\nJo Salamon (26:22): Nice.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:23): In season one. If folks want to go back and listen to her episode, I believe it's episode 15 or 16, so I'd highly encourage you to go listen to Rachel and she kind of shares about how she got into the outdoor industry and some of the challenges, but also opportunities that exist in her world running a PR agency. So", "token_estimate": 458, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jo Salamon"], "start_timestamp": "24:54", "end_timestamp": "26:23", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "d14f11c1-c9bb-495b-b232-7121a726872e", "episode_id": "81a88073-9d06-4800-a461-ab715179006d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484e", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Jo Salamon (26:42): Yeah, they've been great. So excited to work with them. They're a great team, very enthusiastic and just got a lot of energy, a lot of great ideas. And to have what is really a brand that's not that well recognized in the recreation outdoor industry, but still has an iconic history and a background, and to have them help us elevate and tell that story has been really great. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:06): If people want to follow along with you or with Mustang, where can people go to connect?\n\nJo Salamon (27:12): Best thing I think is to follow us on Instagram, which is Mustang Survival. And you'll see the iconic logo with the seahorse if you're not used to us. It's a great Instagram channel. We really share a lot of our stories from our ambassadors, from those end users and the amazing adventures, the amazing places they go in our gear. It's so inspiring. So I love following the channel.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:35): Alright Joe, well thanks again for stopping by and chatting and I hope you have a good rest of your day.\n\nJo Salamon (27:39): Thank you. It's been fun being here.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:41): Alright, bye\n\nJo Salamon (27:42): Bye.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:44): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 351, "speakers": ["Jo Salamon", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:42", "end_timestamp": "27:44", "podcast_index": 42, "episode_name": "Ep 42: Jo Salamon | Preserving Heritage and Saving Lives | Mustang Surviva‪l‬", "slug": "ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-42-jo-salamon-preserving-heritage-and-saving-lives-mustang-survival", "guest_name": "Jo Salamon", "guest_title": "Communications Manager, Mustang Survival", "categories": [], "short_description": "Mustang Survival's iconic seahorse logo is a common sight on the water. For years, Mustang has worked in the commercial marine industry supplying life-saving PFD's and float coats. Recently however they've made a pivot to the recreation industry bringing their experience, heritage, and technical skills honed through government contracts to the everyday user.", "podcast_length": "44:64"}, {"id": "44bb34ff-4ed8-4af0-8894-51d4b97e4754", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (00:01): Yeah. Oh, it's totally true. They literally use human attention as a commodity. I mean, that's how it works. And Facebook is basically a black box. You don't know what the algorithms are doing. You don't know how it works, really, we dump money into this ads manager to throw ads at it, and it's pay to play. If you're not paying for it, you're not getting a return on investment.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole, he born. Today I'm sitting down with Mary Kate Bauman, the Senior marketing strategist at Goal Zero. Mary Kate, how are you?\n\nMary Kate Baumann (01:08): I'm good. How are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:10): First, I'm really excited to jump into our conversation today, but I'm curious to first talk a little bit of background on Goal Zero and your role with the company. So maybe can you give us the quick background on who you guys are and what your role is and how you ended up as a senior marketing strategist?", "token_estimate": 393, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01", "end_timestamp": "01:10", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "a3092002-271b-4ddf-a2ff-edf02d49863b", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (01:28): Yeah, for sure. So Goal Zero started about 10 years ago in the Republic of Congo. Actually, the guy who created the company went there on a trip and saw that there were people there without power, without lights, anything. So it was kind of started as a philanthropic endeavor. And then word got out about our products in the US and people were like, well, I want that for whatever I'm doing, and I could use that camping and whatnot. So it kind of evolved into this company that it is today where you have all of the power products and light products and solar panels to enable people and empower people to really get out further, stay out longer, and do the things that they love doing off grid or on grid during emergencies, during work, whatever they need our products for. (02:33): And so that's kind of where we are today. And now we've kind of grown a lot in the past year alone because of all of the wildfires in California and all of the natural disasters happening around the world. People are seeking our company out more and more, and we've evolved past kind of the outdoor persona that we've had in the past, and now we're more of an emergency preparedness. In addition to the outdoors, we produce power packs, so they're kind of like large battery inverters. We make small ones from a hundred watt hour ones all the way up to 6,000 watt hour products. So the 6,000 watt hour ones can power an entire home for a couple of days. And our a hundred watt hour ones can charge your laptop, charge your phone for a couple of recharges at a time. So we offer a really wide array of products to help people charge and stay powered for whatever they want to be doing.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:39): And you guys also make solar panels to recharge those battery banks. And I also feel like you're really in an exciting industry because I'm assuming battery technology is just getting better and better every year, which means you can do more for less.", "token_estimate": 494, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:28", "end_timestamp": "03:39", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "cf3a5b0d-f7de-43aa-ace0-4becacadc234", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (03:54): So we started with lead acid batteries, and then as time went on, lithium came into the spotlight. And so now all of our batteries are lithium ion. You could still get the lead acid ones, but they're a lot heavier. And so the lithium ions are lighter, they're more powerful. They allow you to stay out longer, which is basically the objective. We have our new product line, the Yeti X line that came out, and they're all more compact and lighter than their predecessors. So as technology is evolving, so are our products, and we're always looking for the next best thing to use to help people power anything that they want.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:40): And your role with the company, what do you oversee? What do you get to work on?\n\nMary Kate Baumann (04:44): So as a senior marketing strategist, I basically run all of the social media channels paid and own. So anywhere from Instagram, Facebook, Pinterest, Twitter, all of the above. If you send a DM to the Instagram account, I'm usually replying, and I'll try and help everyone, but if it's technical question, I'll probably send you to customer support. But yeah, so I do the daily posts. I run all of our ads on our social channels, and so monitoring that, it's a large undertaking, especially as a growing brand. But yeah, it's really, and obviously we are empowered at Goal Zero to explore other avenues in other areas of the company if we want to. So I recently had the opportunity to partake in a large request for proposal for a mass media campaign that we were running. So I was able to create all of the requests, a lot of the documentation that we would send to the agencies that we were working with, and really got my feet wet in that mass media campaign arena, if that makes sense. So we're always asked to not go above and beyond, but if we want to be doing something that we're not currently doing, just stepping up and saying, I want to take a swing at this. And I love that. About Goal Zero.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:17): Did you guys work with the agency struck on that project?", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:54", "end_timestamp": "06:17", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "e83f360b-b0bc-4a2b-836d-2aed8e04a62b", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (06:21): Yes. Yes, we did. So we went through a number of, I think we started out talking to about six or so agencies around the United States, and then ended up with Struc, which is actually really, really fun because they're local to Utah. And so it's great having them so close by, not that we ever met with them in person because of COVID, but in the future, if we're going to work with them, it's really easy to all get into a room together and have some creative discussions and ideation around that. So yeah, they've done campaigns with Snowbird and the Big Five, the Mighty five is what they called their campaign with the National parks in Utah. So yeah, it's cool to see local agencies doing big things for other companies, including ours. Including ours. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (07:14): And the campaign turned out great. I saw the couple spots that you guys ended up creating. They were really well done.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (07:20): Oh, yeah. Oh yeah. We actually used a local production company to do that too. Chad Kirkland is an amazing director and photographer. He's done some pretty big stuff as well. And his idea behind it, having the Yeti centered throughout the whole commercial was just, I mean, it's brilliant, and if you watch it, you can't help but feel like, yeah, this thing is big, and it's a great tool for anyone, for any situation. So\n\nCole Heilborn (07:53): Yeah,\n\nMary Kate Baumann (07:53): It turned out great.", "token_estimate": 366, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:21", "end_timestamp": "07:53", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "27687a13-bbe6-45b5-bddb-1a400266d580", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:55): Awesome. Well, I'm super excited, like I said at the beginning of this, to kind of jump into today's conversation, and this is kind of timely, if you're, I know we connected originally over, well, just marketing in general, but then the social dilemma film came out, and I think we both ended up watching it around similar times when we were talking about where could we take an episode with you, what would we want to talk about? The film came out, we watched it, and then you were like, Hey, I just watched this film. Would this be of interest? And I was like, Hey, I just watched that film. I have so many questions. Yeah, let's talk about it. So for folks who haven't seen it, it's a Netflix documentary. I'm going to read the description from Netflix. It explores the dangerous human impact of social networking with tech experts sounding the alarm on their own creations. It's about an hour and a half. It's really well done, and it raises a lot of interesting questions. And Mary Kate, I'm curious, your role with Goal Zero is, as you described, social media, managing, scheduling, engaging, posting, all of that stuff for someone who's in the industry and does that the day to day, I'm just curious what your initial reaction was to the film.", "token_estimate": 319, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:55", "end_timestamp": "07:55", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "70c7d175-0df7-4dd7-9a24-d046e6e45cd0", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (09:13): Yeah, so I've always had a love hate relationship with social media. I think people who work in it in that industry day in and day out, they like it, obviously, their wellbeing. It is my job, so I am in it all the time and I rely on it for my work. So in one sense, I love that it exists, but on the other, as a personal user of it, I've seen the negative effects of it. They talk a lot about how the algorithms will kind of perpetuate false narratives and it spreads misinformation, and I hate that aspect of it. A prime example is that one of the people that used to work at Facebook said that their algorithms exploit the human's brain's attraction to divisiveness. That's a direct quote. And it said, more and more device content is an effort to gain a user's attention and increase their time on the platform. (10:18): So their algorithms are showing people content that they either don't agree with or agree with and tries and gets them to interact with it more and more and keeps their attention on the platform. And in one sense, that's great for my job because if people are interacting with our content and spending more time on our content and spending more time on our site and then eventually converting and making a purchase, that's perfect. But if it's information that's not necessarily true, or information that's spreading hate or bullying or I don't know, especially in this political climate, it's not necessarily a good thing. And it's scary to say the least, especially knowing what we know now about the 2016 election and Russian interference. I mean, it's kind of crazy to think that something that was created in 2000, what 2006, 2004, could evolve to this monstrous thing that we know as it is today and have an effect in the elections of our country's highest position.", "token_estimate": 456, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "09:13", "end_timestamp": "09:13", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "a0e2e0fc-492b-492d-990a-6fe21f42f65b", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:35): I'm kind of like you, where I never really, I've used social media because I'll call it a tool. I think it's useful. I mean, that's how I ended up connecting with you is over LinkedIn. And so prime example of how it can be helpful, but what I walked away from the documentary, I guess maybe realizing understanding more is that, and they kind of outline this, it's like the difference between a tool is that it doesn't ask to be used. It's there when you need it. And the difference with social media is that it's designed to keep being used and to keep pulling you back. And when you're sitting on the bus with nothing to do, your muscle memory, your mind is like, oh, let me just check Instagram. Oh, let me check LinkedIn. And we've become so used to just killing five minutes at a time, 10 minutes at a time that we don't even realize that we're being drawn into these apps. And I think what I didn't realize, but I realized after watching it is that they're designed to do that. They're designed to keep you coming back for more, which is just, I don't know, the whole time I was like, wow, this is crazy.", "token_estimate": 284, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:35", "end_timestamp": "11:35", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "aec49a55-4ea6-4a10-b9aa-3f60593fb62c", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (12:44): Yeah. Oh, it's totally true. They literally use human attention as a commodity, and that's how it works. And Facebook is basically a black box. You don't know what the algorithms are doing. You don't know how it works, really. We dump money into this ads manager to throw ads at it, and it's pay to play. If you're not paying for it, you're not getting a return on investment. And it's like the more money you funnel into it, the more money you get back out of it. And so it's like the only one of its kind. And you can't see who you're competing with. You don't know who you're serving ads to. It's all behind the curtain, and it's pretty crazy. And to what you said when you're sitting on a bus and you got time to look at it, of course that's what you're going to do. (13:33): And I've been in situations where I've left my phone in the car while I run in somewhere, and if I'm waiting in line, I reach into my pocket looking for that phone, and remember I left in the car. And then you're like, wow, I'm kind of bored just sitting here not doing anything. And you look around and everyone is on their phone, and that's when it hits you. You're like, wow, this is kind of addictive, kind of scary. Think of all the things that you could have gotten done if social media didn't exist. You know what I mean?\n\nCole Heilborn (14:00): Right.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (14:01): Yeah. So on one hand I love it because yeah, it helps you pass time. You can catch up with your friends and your family and all of that good thing, good stuff. But at the same time, 30 minutes, an hour goes by and you're still on the app. I mean, TikTok is a great example of that. Right. Have you downloaded TikTok at all?\n\nCole Heilborn (14:26): So I don't quite understand it. I've been on it a few times, but I haven't gotten hooked on it. I don't really enjoy it.", "token_estimate": 466, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:44", "end_timestamp": "14:26", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "92628bf7-5d23-4469-8cc6-2ae8d52f8f3f", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (14:35): So Vine was very similar Way back in the day when Vine was still in existence, I was asked by my boss to download TikTok to see if there's a market for us there, which I'm not sold on yet. But downloading it was the worst decision I've ever made because I feel like I spend time on TikTok very seldom now, but I'll open the app and then 40 minutes will go by because it's just so easy to digest. You just swipe down, watch a video, swipe down, watch a video. And a lot of them are really funny. And so it's like little prepackaged digestive memes that you can enjoy and spend time and it's like an escape from reality. But yeah, it's dangerous. I'll say, I don't know.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:26): And I guess I don't want this conversation to make it sound like social media is bad, because I don't think it is. I just think there's parts of it that they know human nature and they understand how humans work, and it's been designed in a way that kind of exploits some of our weaknesses. So it obviously can be a great tool, and it's done wonders for businesses. And I mean, it's given you essentially a job because that's what you do. It's a good thing. But I'm curious, what type of questions have you started asking yourself after watching the film about the work that you do in the day-to-day at Goal Zero when you're posting or when you're on social media? Has it made you question anything?", "token_estimate": 355, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:35", "end_timestamp": "15:26", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "2d55c52a-fc39-4c37-b8d7-5ee96b5fdb48", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (16:17): I would say not personally, no, because I think that Goal Zero is pretty black and white. We're a company, and at the end of the day, we're trying to sell products. We also sell a lifestyle, I would say, as well. And I don't think that the content we are producing is a negative for people. If anything, it's showing you what you can attain if you have our product, which isn't necessarily true. You could go out and you can camp and you can go over landing and do all of the things that we're portraying without our product, but we're showing customers how much easier it is with it. And I don't know. I know that the algorithms are such that if you look at this one thing, it has a higher propensity to show you something that's similar. But I don't know that our product is something that's divisive (17:22): Or hot that will get a lot of people fired up about something. So on one hand, no, I realize that that facet of social media is there, that there's that dark curtain that we don't know what happens behind the scenes. But my role in particular, I don't feel like it's adding to that even though it does clearly, because I mean, obviously people are clicking on our ads and the algorithms and the machine is learning about every single piece of that person. If I click on an ad for West Elm because I'm searching for couches, I'm now going to be served ads from Creighton Barrel, Williams, Sonoma, pottery Barn, all of those companies, because it now knows, oh, she's looking for couches, so it follows you everywhere, which is kind of crazy. And there have been times where I know that they say that this isn't true, that the microphone is listening to you and whatnot, but there have been some really weird situations where I'll say something out loud and then I'll get served in an ad for it. And then the same goes for people in your household. If someone in your household said something about a couch or anything benign, I could get ads served to me for that because they know that we live in the same place, which is crazy. They know that based on our habits, that we live in the same dwelling, (19:07): Which blows my mind. It's so smart. It's so smart. Too smart for its own good almost.", "token_estimate": 559, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "16:17", "end_timestamp": "16:17", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "0547d55a-634e-4979-8e89-bbeb23096cd5", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (19:13): I do wonder, especially for the outdoor industry, which is very, I think lot of, like you said, a lot of the marketing is aspirational. It's all about trying to inspire folks to get outdoors. I do think there, in my maybe jaded opinion, sometimes we can make aspirational content and we can say that we're trying to get people outdoors, but we're really just trying to keep them to keep engaging with our content that we're creating. And that's another conversation. But I also think that there's some amazing good out there that can happen that I think the outdoor industry has been known to do in getting people to change things up, get outside, go experience things, and they're using these same tools that are also being used for negative things.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (20:01): Yeah, totally. I mean, a perfect example is i's opt-out side campaign for Black Friday. Instead of going in stores and buying everything that you possibly could want, and all the Christmas presents and Hanukkah presents and holiday presents for your friends and family, they're encouraging everyone to go outside. And that's huge. That hashtag still has over 20 million posts on it, and people use it to this day just in a normal outdoor post on top of a mountain opt outside, hashtag opt outside. So that's a perfect example of an outdoor brand using the tool for good, not outdoor, but another positive campaign that I can recall in the past couple of years was the a OS challenge between the Bucket and then the Challenge where they filmed the video and everyone was super still. Do you remember that one? Yes,\n\nCole Heilborn (21:01): I do. I'm trying to remember what that was called.", "token_estimate": 423, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "19:13", "end_timestamp": "21:01", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "f7a28331-5f0b-4af2-8de5-275a29302c66", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (21:04): I don't remember which. Maybe that means it wasn't as effective as it should have been, right? If we don't remember what it was actually for. But I mean, those are some of the more notable ones for great causes. And then Facebook now, if it's your birthday and you want to get money for your charity of choice, they now have an easy donate to my cause button. So yeah, social media is definitely good in some ways, and GoFundMe was probably started crowdsourcing. That's a social type of platform as well. And then I'm trying to think of other outdoor companies. Yeah, I mean the North Face does, the walls are meant for climbing. I mean, that was a political move by the North Face, but I mean, it was really effective. And they did a in-person event, live activations of creating walls in different cities that people can climb all branded North Face, and it made a political statement and it was really effective. It at least made people think about it. But yeah, there have been some really good ones. Opt outside I think is probably the biggest one that I could think of.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:23): Yeah. What do you think it takes from being on the brand side? What does it take to create one of those positive social movements?", "token_estimate": 315, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:04", "end_timestamp": "22:23", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "28b2c0d2-663c-49b6-bcd4-92ee045d8a2a", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (22:32): Oh, man. I think it takes guts first and foremost. I'm not just saying that. I honestly think that brands taking strong positions on either side is a really gutsy and brave thing to do because you're potentially losing half of your followers or half of your brand loyalty, and you see that all the time people take a stand, companies will take a stand for something and they'll get responses like, keep politics out of it. Unfollowing, unsubscribing. I'm not buying from you again. This happened at Backcountry a couple months ago after they actually, it wasn't really a political statement, but I know that they went after a couple of companies with the name backcountry in them, and people were up in arms about it, and a lot of people started boycotting them. I'm not sure that it affected them to the degree that some people would've liked, but yeah, I think it takes guts first and foremost. I think it takes the perfect vacuum of a situation. You got to see what's happening in culture and society and then creating something that has to do with that and then executing it. I think it takes a lot of effort and to right time, the right place, the right messaging as well. (24:06): And yeah, I think what made Optout Outside so successful was that it was the complete opposite of what everyone else is doing. Instead of asking people to come in and shop and shop the deals, they're literally asking people to stay away from their stores. They literally closed every single one of their stores. And I think that's the type of guts that it takes. That's the perfect time and the perfect message.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:33): Considering people's general perception of social media, which might or might not on a broad scale, be influenced by this film, how do you think just the way brands use social media and how brands work with ambassadors on social media, do you think that things will change in the future as you're looking at stuff day to day and then projecting to the future?", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:32", "end_timestamp": "24:33", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "428a4c2e-9e75-4ad2-af43-2e145e4e31ca", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (25:04): One thing that I've noticed recently is that people are demanding more from ambassadors and influencers because they do have a reach that's larger than most. I think that a lot of people are demanding them to be better for the environment, especially in our industry, the outdoor industry better for the environment, more vocal in their views on the environment, more vocal in their views on politics. I think that there's a larger demand for people who are more authentic in that facet of their lives, and whether that's really adhering to leave no trace or standing up and calling their state representatives to protect lands. I think that followers and users of social media are really expecting more from influencers that they're following. I think in one way that'll change, I think that users will start demanding that brands work with influencers that are more authentic in that way and stop supporting influencers who are not obeying leave no trace rules or rules of the national parks or BLM. And yeah, I think that it's, it's going to take a lot of due diligence on brands, work on brand side to really examine who they're working with in the future.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:40): How do you think social media has influenced or changed people's perceptions about the outdoors getting outside having an adventure? Has it changed people's perception of it?", "token_estimate": 347, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:04", "end_timestamp": "26:40", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "608629bd-9696-4c15-b1d8-e46bb6eb35d4", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (26:52): I think advertising and social media has really helped people get outside more. I think that taking photos in poppy fields, it's terrible for the environment, but it's made other people want to aspire to do something like that. So they'll go seek out these sunflower fields in poppy fields and go to take photos and therefore be outside. A perfect example of that is there's, in the Salt Flats, there is a aqueduct of sorts out in Utah that people have been traveling to and swimming in and stand up paddle boarding in to get a really epic shot from above using a drone. (27:39): And I've heard mixed reviews on it. People are like, this is so cool. But then there are those people that are saying, don't do this. Don't tag where you are. They're very polarizing sides. So on one hand, I think that gatekeeping sucks. I don't, I'm a fly fisher, and I hate when I DM a fly fisher like an influencer, for example, and I say, Hey, where was this? And they're like, oh, sorry. I'm not telling you. I hate that. First of all, I'm a female fly fisher, which there's a huge underrepresentation of females in that sport, and also I am just starting out and I'd love to access some really cool places that I wouldn't otherwise know about. You know what I mean? (28:31): But on the other hand, I get it because they're trying to protect it. They don't want that area to be overfished or destroyed. I don't know. It's such a dilemma for me. I feel like I understand why people are very protective of their outdoor lands. Alabama Hills is a perfect example. I think that a lot of people have seen photos of that online and have been like, we want to go there. And I've heard from people that it's so overrun now that no one is following leave, no trace. There's human feces left in areas. And so on that hand, I totally get it. Don't share where these open places are. But on the other hand, it's like, but why wouldn't you want people that normally wouldn't be able to experience this, be able to go out and experience it? So yeah, I don't know the answer. I think that social media has made the outdoors a lot more accessible for people that otherwise wouldn't have that access. But I also think it's destroyed a lot of places. So I don't know the answer.", "token_estimate": 568, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "26:52", "end_timestamp": "26:52", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "2b2a06ac-5c2d-408c-9874-bf1ef9980e19", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:47): Yeah, it's a balance between inspiration and education and responsibility. It's great to get people out there, but you have people need to get out there and the right way and be respectful. I don't remember where, but somewhere in Oregon, there was this super popular place that people would love to go and take photos at. It was on the coast, and the subject was like this rock, and this rock was balanced, very precariously, the wind and the waves that kind of worn it all down. So it was this big rock being held up by this little pedestal, and it became this super popular Instagram destination. It was like a bucket list shot, but at some point it became so popular that someone found it, and then they decided that they were going to go tip the rock over. So they pushed this rock over, broke the base. It was this, I don't know, it was a few, couple years ago, an Instagram community, influencer community got all outrage and rightfully, but yeah, it's like you make it all accessible, and then there's those people out there that abuse those places, and then it kind of ruins it for everyone because now that place doesn't exist anymore.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (31:02): Totally. Totally. That actually happened in southern Utah. I forget it was either, I think it was a national park or a state park. There's a bunch of hoodoos. Do you know what a hoodoo is?\n\nCole Heilborn (31:14): No.", "token_estimate": 354, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "29:47", "end_timestamp": "31:14", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "ee6bf29b-8690-4ac9-a55c-b67177491421", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (31:15): So there are rock formations where they're really tall and thin, and then there are big boulders sitting on the top. So there was a hoodoo down in Southern Utah that was pretty exactly like you were talking about this rock in a really precarious position. But it was really cool. And there was a Boy scout troop that went down there and they said, oh, this seems dangerous. So they pushed the rock over and they were fined thousands of dollars because it's protected and people were pissed. Boy, Scouts of America got a lot of flack for that. Like you can't just be everyone's savior. You know what I mean? You can't just destroy nature and the millions of years that it took for Mother Nature to literally form this rock in the perfect shape, sitting precariously like that just to knock it over because you think it's dangerous. But yeah, same thing. I hate when stuff like that happens, and I hate that, especially vandalism. I hate that people will write on rocks or spray paint rocks. I think it's just, I don't know. It's really when you see that, especially in a beautiful vista, you're like, this is so pretty. And then some asshole just said, MK plus so-and-so equals love. It's just like, why? What is the point\n\nCole Heilborn (32:58): That, I mean to kind of circle it all back home, getting people outside, getting people to experience these places that they might not otherwise be able to find or access. Those are all great things. That's what social media, you talked about, the good. Those are some of the good things that it can do. But on the flip side, you've got people then who find those places go out and just want to ruin. And so I wish we could have the answers to how should social media work? How do we do this the right way? I guess maybe what all you can do is just talk about it and see how other people feel.", "token_estimate": 467, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:15", "end_timestamp": "32:58", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "41d311c1-5daa-4c71-9e94-a4e23c8f2083", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Mary Kate Baumann (33:38): Totally. Totally. I think there's something to be said about educating. There are some really good Instagram accounts out there that are really good at educating people on leave no trace values and how to be responsible and recreate outside. But then I think that there are accounts that are doing a really shit job at that. Like the call out culture that persists now where people will just berate others for doing things that they deem as bad is just, it's insidious and it creates so much vitriol. And I don't know, there's this one Instagram account in particular that just makes a point of going after brands and going after people who are not practicing leave no trace. And I don't think that it does anything for the conversation. I think it just creates a lot more hate than anything else.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:36): Yeah. Well, we could all use a little less divisiveness. That wouldn't hurt.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (34:43): Yeah. Yeah, a hundred percent. Yeah. I think especially today, just saying, I think a lot of people could use a lot of more kindness nowadays.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:53): Yep. Absolutely. Well, Mary Kate, we're about running out of time. I appreciate you taking the time to chat. I know this is kind of, it's not necessarily like a gloomy subject. I think it's an interesting subject and I appreciate you taking the time to share your thoughts and being okay with both of us not knowing how we've necessarily feel or having all the right answers to the difficult conversation of social media and the good and the bad and the ugly.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (35:23): Yeah, for sure. There's a lot of good, but there's also a lot of ugly, so yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:29): Well, if people want to follow along with Goal Zero, if they want to follow along with you personally, see your latest fly fishing adventures, where can people tune in?\n\nMary Kate Baumann (35:39): So at Goal Zero, that's Goal Zero, and then it's at Mary kate.id, so like InDesign, if that makes sense.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:49): Okay.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (35:49): Two Rs. Two. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Mary Kate Baumann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:38", "end_timestamp": "35:49", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "30ab3009-6e45-4560-ad12-87a153b6b853", "episode_id": "e3bf6e18-a9c6-40c5-8811-7da6a5903d60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4852", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:51): Mary Kate, thank you again. I hope you have a good rest of your day and hopefully you can get out and enjoy the outdoors this weekend.\n\nMary Kate Baumann (35:58): Thanks, you too. It was so fun to do this. Thank you so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:02): Absolutely. Alright, take care. Bye. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 130, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mary Kate Baumann"], "start_timestamp": "35:51", "end_timestamp": "36:02", "podcast_index": 43, "episode_name": "Ep 43: Mary Kate Baumann | The (huge) Social Dilemma | GOAL ZER‪O‬", "slug": "ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-43-mary-kate-baumann-the-huge-social-dilemma-goal-zero-6054d", "guest_name": "Mary Kate Baumann", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Strategist, GOAL ZERO", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Mary Kate Baumann, the Senior Marketing Strategist at GOAL ZERO shares her thoughts on the film and the questions she now asks herself both personally and professionally about the role social media plays in the outdoor industry.", "podcast_length": "44:65"}, {"id": "fc5a8581-f510-4b45-b140-609ef685eeeb", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Kyle McCann (00:00): Really my passion is connecting people with the outdoors. And if it's through brands that do that for them even better, and especially brands that protect the outdoors space that they're focused on.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:15): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Kyle McCann, the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles, USA. Kyle, how are you?\n\nKyle McCann (00:57): Howdy Cole. I'm doing pretty good.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00): Yeah, pleasure to be chatting with you today. I feel like we finally pinned you down. You've been on the road for forever and you're in one place today.\n\nKyle McCann (01:08): Yeah, it's been since the end of September. We've been traveling from San Diego over to the East coast and taking the long route, the north route and had a fantastic ride and not ride on the bicycle, but drive, and it's been moving around ever since. Yeah, you're right. Hard to pin me down in one spot.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:33): No, it's awesome. You guys are looking for a place to settle down, right? That's what kind of spawned the whole road trip is to figure out where do we want to live?\n\nKyle McCann (01:40): Yeah, exactly. So I'm originally from the east coast, well Florida, some people don't call that the East Coast. And I've been, I was in California for the past six years and finally getting a little bit older, ready to move on with life and heading back to the East Coast. And we're finally, finally here.", "token_estimate": 518, "speakers": ["Kyle McCann", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:40", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}, {"id": "63aaa8e0-a3ae-4ecd-9df5-ebec9a659d03", "episode_id": "e9b93f57-4ba4-4f41-992a-ecbb71dce605", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4854", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:02): I'm excited to chat today and learn a little bit more about your story and your career path and just kind of some of the adventures that you've had along the way. But let's first talk a little bit about Canyon. You guys obviously make bicycles, but I'd love to know more about the company and kind of your role within the company.\n\nKyle McCann (02:19): Yeah, so Canyon's motto I think is probably our most important thing. We inspire riders to ride is what we say. So most if you're in the bike industry, likely chance that you've heard of Canyon before, but we make mountain bikes, road bikes, gravel bikes, triathlon, cross country, pretty much anything across the board on bikes we build. It's a German based company and actually direct consumers since day one. So providing bikes to a broad range of riders at a value that's really unmatched outside of direct to consumer. So it's really a cool situation that we're in. Being direct to consumer, especially in this specific time right now is incredible. We're in a pandemic, I don't know if we can mention that right now, but it's pretty wild times and I don't want to wish anything positive on the pandemic, but bike sales have gone through the roof for every bike company and using it not just as a form of recreation but as a form of transportation has been really crucial. (03:36): Taking away cars, taking away public transportation. People have really leaned into the road bike, the gravel bike, the city bike, the fitness bike and canyon as a whole. We've done a really good job during this time to provide that different means of transportation. So canyon's been around for, I want to say it's almost 30 years at this point globally started in Germany, like I mentioned, and has expanded to countries that, I can't remember exactly how many, but it's a lot of countries. I personally work for the US side, and we've actually only been in town for about a little over three years now. Launched in 2017, and we're based in Carlsbad, California. And we actually, we ship our bikes from Chino, California right down the road from Carlsbad. So when you're purchasing a bike from Canyon USA, you're buying the bike directly from the us and that's unlike any of the other Canyon subsidiaries or countries, like I mentioned, direct to consumer. (04:55): So really we get to focus very strongly on engineering, on bringing the value to the customer because we don't have that middle man connection. We don't necessarily say it's a bad thing, but we're able to really provide the best bike for the best price. So typically we're able to save around 10 to 20% or give the savings back to the consumer by about 10 to 20% on any bike. And then we also offer 30 day try it. So basically you can try the bike for 30 days, it doesn't fit, you send it back. We offer financing. We work with our partner Velo Fix to assemble the bikes if you don't feel comfortable assembling the bikes, but we also basically set up every bike before we send it to you and make it as absolutely easy as possible to once it hits your doorstep, assemble it on your own. (05:57): It's basically a handlebar, a front tire and a few other really small things like seat posts. But what most of the people on the customer service team would say is if you can change your tire, you can build your bike. So it's really cool. And my role is, like we mentioned prior, a digital marketing manager that encompasses a lot when we first started talking earlier, touched on what I've been learning about in the most recent times. I'm looking at everything from an omnichannel approach and trying to figure out the path of least resistance for every customer and help guide them through that journey digitally. And that's also brought me to how we can bring them to that journey handheld as well. So integrating customer service, integrating social media, integrating athletes, influencers, events, when events come back, so on and so forth, and bring that into a digital space. (07:04): So on a day-to-day basis, I'm managing our paid social, our paid search, all of our performance marketing channels, our content marketing. I don't actually create, I don't take photos or take video, but I'm optimizing for the search engine to be as happy as possible to lead us at the top of the page. And then marketing automation is one of my biggest tasks on a day-to-day basis. So figuring out once we contact a customer or we get their subscription to a newsletter, how do we bring them down the path again to least resistance on purchasing? And then how do we guide them to be a canyon advocate for a long time? And what we talk about a lot now is kind of owning their garage. So if you ride a road bike eventually you'll want to ride a mountain bike and so on and so forth. And we hope that Canyon is filling your garage day over day.", "token_estimate": 1216, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kyle McCann"], "start_timestamp": "02:02", "end_timestamp": "02:19", "podcast_index": 44, "episode_name": "Ep 44: Kyle McCann | Jumping Ship | Canyon Bicycles US‪A‬", "slug": "ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-44-kyle-mccann-jumping-ship-canyon-bicycles-usa", "guest_name": "Kyle McCann", "guest_title": "Digital marketing manager, Canyon Bicycles USA", "categories": [], "short_description": "Kyle McCann is the digital marketing manager at Canyon Bicycles USA. In this episode, we talk about his winding career path from sport fishing, to Facebook, to Canyon. He shares about the lessons he's learned personally and professionally along the wa", "podcast_length": "44:66"}]
